window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"AUTOMATA Wiki","g":"Start","u":"index.html","h":[["where-to-go","Where to go"],["oberon-the-editor","Oberon (the editor)"],["python","Python"],["the-two-rules-of-the-frame-model","The two rules of the frame model"],["conventions","Conventions"]],"x":"AUTOMATA Wiki Welcome to the AUTOMATA documentation wiki — the complete guide to making 2D and 3D games with a Rust engine core and Python gameplay code. You can make games two ways, and both use the same engine and file format: Code-first: a game is an ordinary Python program. You write a Game , stage Entity objects into a Scene , and react to input every frame. Oberon, the editor: build scenes visually, attach Python behaviour scripts, press Play, and tune graphics live. See Oberon . Copy Where to go # New here? Follow the tutorials . The Oberon track takes you from an empty scene to a tested, shippable game in about two hours. Oberon (the editor) # I want to… Read Install and open the editor Oberon Learn every panel and how to navigate The interface Write behaviour scripts Scripting Place and drive cameras Cameras Tune lighting, fog, and tone mapping, and test graphics Graphics Use the built-in code editor and language servers The code editor Reuse objects and add custom data Prefabs & attributes Ship your game for Windows and Linux Exporting your game Look up a shortcut Keyboard shortcuts Python # I want to… Read Install the engine and open my first window Getting Started Learn everything , step by step, building a real game Zero to Hero Look up an exact class, method, or parameter API Reference Solve a specific problem (double jump, pause, spawn waves…) Cookbook Understand the update loop and the frame model Guide: The Game Loop Build levels out of entities and components Guide: Scenes & Entities Draw things, light them, fog and tone mapping Guide: Rendering & Lights Follow, orbit, rail, and shake cameras Guide: Cameras Handle keyboard, mouse, and gamepads Guide: Input Add gravity, collisions, and character movement Guide: Physics Pass messages and store game state Guide: Events & Resources Structure a big game with classes Guide: OOP Style Test my game headlessly in CI Guide: Testing Know how many scripted entities a frame can afford Guide: Performance Organize a project past one file Guide: Project Structure Save and load scenes as files Guide: Scene Files The two rules of the frame model # Everything else in this wiki follows from these two rules: Reads see the start of the frame. Inside on_update , ctx is a snapshot of the world as the frame began. (One exception: after you write a transform, reads of that entity return your pending write.) Writes apply after your callback. Setting a transform, despawning a coin, or sending an event is buffered and becomes visible next frame. That's it. No locks, no partial states, no data races with the engine. Conventions # Angles are radians in Transform.rotation_euler , degrees in Camera3D(fov=...) . Colors are (r, g, b, a) float tuples in the 0.0..=1.0 range. Input names are typed enums — at.Key.SPACE , at.GamepadButton.SOUTH — but plain strings ( \"space\" ) work everywhere too. Headless mode ( headless=True ) runs the full engine without a window or GPU: the same loop, the same physics, no rendering. It's how you test."},{"t":"Getting Started","g":"Start","u":"getting-started.html","h":[["requirements","Requirements"],["install","Install"],["your-first-window-15-lines","Your first window (15 lines)"],["the-two-problems-everyone-hits-once","The two problems everyone hits once"],["headless-mode-no-window","Headless mode (no window)"],["next-step","Next step"]],"x":"Getting Started This page takes you from a fresh checkout to a window on screen, and ends with the two problems everyone hits once. Requirements # Requirement Why Python 3.11+ The gameplay language A Rust toolchain The engine core is Rust A display/GPU (for windowed mode) Headless mode needs neither patchelf (Linux only) Wheel repair during builds Install # From a checkout of the repository: Copy That one script compiles the Rust workspace, builds the Python wheel with maturin, and installs it into your active virtualenv. For a full build-plus-verify pass: Copy Your first window (15 lines) # Save as hello.py and run python hello.py : Copy A window opens, a green cube sits in it (lit by a default light), and Esc closes it. game.run returns the number of frames rendered when the loop ends. The two problems everyone hits once # 1. \"The window opens but I see nothing.\" A 3D scene is invisible until it has both of these: a Camera3D entity (the first camera entity wins each frame), and at least one entity with both Transform and MeshRenderer . Check those before anything else. (For atmosphere, add a DirectionalLight ; without one, a default shadowless light is used.) 2. \"It says module 'automata' has no attribute ... \" Your installed wheel is older than your checkout. Rebuild and reinstall: Copy Headless mode (no window) # For tests, CI, and servers, run the same game without a window or GPU: Copy MeshRenderer components are inert in headless mode — physics, input logic, and events all behave identically. Every example supports a --headless flag; see Testing . Next step # Zero to Hero — build a complete platformer, one concept at a time."},{"t":"Tutorials","g":"Start","u":"tutorials/index.html","h":[["oberon-track","Oberon track"],["python-track","Python track"]],"x":"Tutorials These are hands-on walkthroughs, and each one ends with something that runs. Do them in order the first time; later tutorials assume the earlier ones. Oberon track # You build in the editor and write small Python behaviour scripts. Tutorial You'll learn Time Your first scene Creating, moving, and lighting objects; a camera; Play; saving 10 min Coin collector Behaviour scripts, attributes, scoring, the Console, hot reload 25 min Third-person camera Follow, orbit, look-ahead, and sprint zoom with ctx.camera 20 min Camera rail & intro shot Keyframed camera moves, easing, handing off to gameplay 15 min Lighting & mood The Graphics tab: sun, environment, tone mapping, fog, test scenes 15 min From Oberon to Python at.run_scene , headless tests in CI, shipping a game 15 min A first-person shooter Kenney's CC0 models and sounds, import settings, mouse look, ray-cast shooting, a HUD, export 40 min The finished project from these tutorials is in examples/oberon/coin_run/ . Open level.ron in Oberon to compare with your own: Copy Tutorial Finished result in coin_run/ Your first scene level.ron : ground, ledge, player, sun, environment, camera Coin collector scripts/coin.py , scripts/rules.py , scripts/player.py ; six coins, one worth 5 Third-person camera scripts/follow_camera.py : follow, Q / E orbit, look-ahead, sprint zoom Camera rail & intro shot Shot 1 – Shot 3 markers and scripts/camera_rail.py , attached to Main Camera Lighting & mood level.ron (afternoon), level_dusk.ron , level_night.ron From Oberon to Python main.py and tests/test_level.py The shooter is a separate project, examples/oberon/fps/ ( Blaster Arena ). build_level.py regenerates the three scene files from code. CI plays the project in both Oberon's script runner and the engine, and checks that the scripts on these pages match the files. Python track # You write games as plain Python programs, with no editor. Tutorial You'll learn Getting started Install, first window, first cube Zero to hero A complete platformer: scenes, input, physics, cameras, events, testing Cookbook Short recipes: double jump, pause, spawn waves, screen shake Guide: Cameras Follow, orbit, rails, shake, zoom in code Guide: Rendering & Lights Primitives, the sun, Environment, quality presets Every Zero to Hero module and exercise solution is a runnable file in examples/python/zero_to_hero/ . Every Cookbook recipe works together in examples/python/cookbook_platformer.py . CI runs them all headless: Copy The two tracks meet: scenes made in Oberon load in Python ( at.Scene.load ), and scenes built in Python open in Oberon ( scene.save(\"level.ron\") )."},{"t":"Tutorial: Your first scene","g":"Tutorials","u":"tutorials/first-scene.html","h":[["1-start-clean","1. Start clean"],["2-a-floor","2. A floor"],["3-some-objects","3. Some objects"],["4-light","4. Light"],["5-a-player","5. A player"],["6-a-camera","6. A camera"],["7-play","7. Play"],["8-save","8. Save"],["what-you-learned","What you learned"]],"x":"Tutorial: Your first scene Goal: build a small level in Oberon, light it, place a camera, play it, and save it. No code yet. You need: Oberon running ( cargo run -p oberon --release ). See Oberon . 1. Start clean # Oberon opens with a starter scene: a sun, a ground, a cube, a player, and three crates. Choose File → New Scene (Ctrl+N) to get an empty one instead. The Hierarchy on the left is now empty. 2. A floor # Click + at the top of the Hierarchy and choose Cube . In the Inspector, set Transform → Scale to 20, 0.2, 20 and Position Y to -0.1 . Click a field and type, or drag the X / Y / Z label left and right to scrub. Click the name field at the top of the Inspector and rename the cube Ground . F2 also works. Make it solid: choose Add Component → Rigidbody and set Body Type to Static . Then choose Add Component → Collider ; the collider sizes itself to the mesh. ( + → Plane makes a flat visual-only quad. That's good for decoration, but use a cube when things need to stand on it.) 3. Some objects # + → Cube . Press W for the Move gizmo and drag the red arrow to move the cube along X. Press Ctrl+D a few times to duplicate it, and spread the copies around. Select one cube and click the Color swatch under Mesh Renderer . Pick a colour, or type a hex value such as #E8613A . Press R for Scale and stretch one cube into a tall pillar. Made a mistake? Ctrl+Z undoes it. Every edit is one step, including a whole gizmo drag. 4. Light # Choose + → Directional Light . A sun icon appears in the view, and the objects get light and shadows. Open the Graphics tab (right dock, beside Inspector). Under Sun : drag Elevation down to about 25 for long evening shadows drag Azimuth around until the shadows fall where you like them 5. A player # Choose + → Player . You get an orange capsule-sized box with a Character Controller . In Play mode it walks with WASD, jumps with Space, and sprints with Shift. No script is needed for that. Move it up a little so it isn't stuck inside the floor. A Y position of 0.9 stands it on the ground. 6. A camera # Orbit the view (drag empty space) and zoom (wheel) until you have a nice shot from behind and above the player. Then choose + → Camera . The new camera is placed exactly where you're looking from: a frustum pyramid shows what it sees a preview in the bottom-right corner shows its live view Orbit away and the frustum stays put. Click Look Through in the Inspector to jump back into the camera's view. Adjust the shot, then click Align to View to move the camera there. 7. Play # Press ▶ (Ctrl+P). The view switches to your camera. WASD moves the player relative to the camera, and Space jumps. Press ■ to stop. Everything returns to exactly where it was. The camera doesn't follow the player yet; you'll script that in Third-person camera . If you delete the camera, Play falls back to the editor view following the player. 8. Save # Ctrl+S asks for a file name. Save as my_game/level.ron (make a new folder for it). The title bar shows the name, and the unsaved-changes dot disappears. The file is plain text you can read. It now shows up in Files at the bottom of the left sidebar. Open it there, or in any editor: Copy What you learned # Hierarchy + creates objects; the Inspector edits them. W / E / R gizmos, Ctrl+D duplicate, Ctrl+Z undo. The sun's direction lives in Graphics → Sun. A Camera decides what Play shows, and Look Through / Align to View frame it. Scenes are .ron text files. Next: Coin collector . Make it a game with Python."},{"t":"Tutorial: Coin collector","g":"Tutorials","u":"tutorials/coin-collector.html","h":[["1-a-coin","1. A coin"],["2-the-coin-script","2. The coin script"],["3-hot-reload","3. Hot reload"],["4-many-coins-different-values","4. Many coins, different values"],["5-keeping-score","5. Keeping score"],["6-your-own-player-script-optional","6. Your own player script (optional)"],["what-you-learned","What you learned"]],"x":"Tutorial: Coin collector Goal: turn your first scene into a game. Coins spin and bob, the player collects them, a score counts up, and you win when they're all gone. You'll learn: behaviour scripts ( start / update ) attributes reading other entities the Console hot reload Start from: the scene from Your first scene . Open it with Ctrl+O. Or open the finished version, examples/oberon/coin_run/level.ron , and follow along. 1. A coin # + → Cube , renamed Coin 1 . Scale it to 0.5, 0.5, 0.12 : a flat disc-ish block. Colour it gold: #FFC733 . Set its Y position to 1 so it floats at chest height. 2. The coin script # With the coin selected, choose Add Component → Script… and type scripts/coin.py . Oberon creates the file from a template and opens it in the code editor. Replace the contents with: Copy Save it (Ctrl+S), press Esc to return to the scene, and press Play . The coin spins and bobs. Walk into it, and it disappears with +1 in the Console. What's going on: start runs once. We remember the coin's starting position in entity.state , a private dict that lasts for the whole Play session. update runs every frame. ctx.time.elapsed drives the motion, so it runs at the same speed at any frame rate. ctx.scene[\"Player\"] finds another entity by name. entity.attributes[\"collected\"] is a value other scripts can read . That's how the score will know. 3. Hot reload # Keep Play running. In the code editor, change t * 2.5 to t * 8.0 and save. Within half a second the Console says Reloaded coin.py , and the coin spins faster. You don't need to restart. Now break it on purpose: on that same line, change entity.transform to entity.transfrom and save. The Console shows the error with a coin.py:22 link. That coin pauses; the rest of the game keeps running. Fix the typo and save, and it resumes. 4. Many coins, different values # Stop. Select the coin and press Ctrl+D five times. The copies share scripts/coin.py ; each one gets its own entity and state . Spread them out, and put one somewhere hard to reach, such as on top of a pillar. Give that hard-to-reach coin a bigger reward: In the Inspector, choose Attributes → + Add attribute . Name: value , type Int , value 5 . The script already reads entity.attributes.get('value', 1) , so coins without the attribute are worth 1. 5. Keeping score # Score belongs to the game, not to any coin. Choose + → Empty and name it Rules . Attach a new script, scripts/rules.py : Copy Play and collect everything. The Console counts up and celebrates at the end. Why doesn't each coin add to the score itself? Every script reads the start-of-frame state, and writes apply after all scripts run. If two coins were collected in the same frame, each would read score = 3 and write 4 . Having one writer per value avoids that. Coins report their own state, and the rules script adds it up. 6. Your own player script (optional) # The built-in Player controller is fine in Oberon. A script makes the game behave the same outside the editor too (see From Oberon to Python ). Attach scripts/player.py to the Player. Use the version in examples/oberon/coin_run/scripts/player.py : it walks relative to the camera, sprints, jumps, and has gravity. A character with a script skips the built-in controller. The heart of it: Copy What you learned # A behaviour script has start(entity, ctx) and update(entity, ctx) . entity.state is private memory; entity.attributes is shared, shown in the Inspector, and saved. Find other entities with ctx.scene[\"Name\"] or by iterating ctx.scene . Save to hot-reload; errors pause only the script that failed. Have one writer per value. Next: Third-person camera . Make the camera chase the player."},{"t":"Tutorial: Third-person camera","g":"Tutorials","u":"tutorials/third-person-camera.html","h":[["step-1-follow","Step 1: Follow"],["step-2-orbit-with-q--e","Step 2: Orbit with Q / E"],["step-3-look-ahead","Step 3: Look ahead"],["step-4-sprint-zoom","Step 4: Sprint zoom"],["tuning","Tuning"],["the-finished-script","The finished script"]],"x":"Tutorial: Third-person camera Goal: a camera that chases the player smoothly, orbits with Q / E, looks ahead when moving, and widens its view while sprinting. You'll build it in four small steps, playing after each one. Start from: the coin collector scene, which needs a Player and a Camera (see Cameras ). Select the camera, choose Add Component → Script… , and name it scripts/follow_camera.py . Step 1: Follow # Copy Play. The camera keeps 3 m above and 8 m behind (+Z) the player, always looking at them, and catches up smoothly when they move. follow(entity, offset=(0, 3, 8), smooth=8.0, look_height=0.6) does three things every frame: Computes the goal: the player's position plus offset . Moves part of the way there. That fraction is 1 − e^(−smooth·dt) , which feels the same at any frame rate. Higher smooth means snappier, and 0 snaps instantly. Aims at the player, look_height metres above their origin. Try smooth=2 for a lazy cinematic follow, or offset=(0, 10, 4) for a top-down view. Step 2: Orbit with Q / E # The offset doesn't have to be fixed. Rotate it around the player with a yaw angle kept in entity.state : Copy Play and hold Q. The camera swings around the player. WASD stays camera-relative as you orbit: The built-in player controller reads the camera's facing automatically. If you wrote your own player.py from the coin tutorial, it does the same from ctx.camera.position and ctx.camera.target . Step 3: Look ahead # A camera that stares at the player's back hides what's coming. Aim a little ahead of the player in the direction they're moving. You can work out the velocity from the position change between frames: Copy follow aims at the player first; calling look_at afterwards replaces the aim. The lead is smoothed separately, so starting and stopping don't jolt the view. Step 4: Sprint zoom # A wider field of view feels faster. Ease the FOV toward a goal: Copy Tuning # Feel Settings Tight action DISTANCE=6 , HEIGHT=2.5 , smooth=10 Relaxed exploration DISTANCE=10 , HEIGHT=4.5 , smooth=4 , LOOK_AHEAD=0.6 Top-down offset=(0, 14, 6) , no orbit Side-scroller Keep Z fixed (see Cameras recipes ) Use Look Through on the camera during Edit mode to check the starting framing, since the first frame of Play starts from where the camera is placed. The finished script # All four steps together, with one small function per step: Copy ctx.input.axis(\"q\", \"e\") is shorthand for float(pressed(\"e\")) - float(pressed(\"q\")) . It's in the example project as scripts/follow_camera.py . The finished level runs the intro from the next tutorial first. To skip it, attach scripts/follow_camera.py to Main Camera instead of camera_rail.py . Next: Camera rail & intro shot ."},{"t":"Tutorial: Camera rail & intro shot","g":"Tutorials","u":"tutorials/camera-rail.html","h":[["1-mark-the-shots-in-the-editor","1. Mark the shots in the editor"],["2-the-rail-script","2. The rail script"],["how-it-works","How it works"],["variations","Variations"]],"x":"Tutorial: Camera rail & intro shot Goal: when Play starts, the camera flies over the level for a few seconds, showing off the coins, and then hands control to the third-person follow camera smoothly. You'll learn: keyframes easing curves using empty entities as editable shot markers blending between two camera behaviours 1. Mark the shots in the editor # Hard-coding positions means you can't see them. Use entities instead: + → Empty , renamed Shot 1 . An Empty only needs a position, so move it to where the camera should be for the opening shot. Add an attribute look of type Vec3 , set to the point the shot should look at. Repeat for Shot 2 and Shot 3 . Shots play in name order. Put the last one where the follow camera starts (behind the player, at the camera's own position), so the hand-off is seamless. Quick way to place a marker: frame the shot, then press + → Camera . It lands at your eye position. Copy its Eye into the Empty's position and its Look At into look . Then delete the temporary camera. 2. The rail script # An entity runs one script, so select Main Camera , remove its Script component, and attach a new one, scripts/camera_rail.py . The end of the rail script brings the orbiting follow camera back. Copy Play. The camera glides through the shots and then settles in behind the player. Press Space during the flyover to skip it. The finished level has three shots and this script, in scripts/camera_rail.py . To add Steps 3 and 4 from the previous tutorial (look-ahead and sprint zoom) after the hand-off, copy look_ahead and sprint_zoom from follow_camera.py and call them after cam.follow . How it works # Keyframes are data in the scene. Moving a Shot entity in the editor changes the flyover without touching code. Designers can tweak it. smoothstep eases each segment in and out. Linear interpolation would jerk at every keyframe; smoothstep has zero speed at both ends. The hand-off reuses follow , but ramps its smooth from 1 (lazy) to 8 (normal) over BLEND seconds. The camera drifts from the last shot to its follow position instead of cutting. done_at remembers when the rail ended, so skipping blends the same way. Everything keys off ctx.time.elapsed , so the shot takes the same time on any machine. Variations # Skip with any key: the script skips on Space. To skip on the first movement key instead: Copy Catmull-Rom curves: with four or more shots, a spline passes through every key without stopping at each one. Replace lerp and smoothstep with: Copy Dolly zoom (the \"Vertigo\" shot): move the camera back while narrowing the field of view, keeping the subject the same size: Copy Next: Lighting & mood ."},{"t":"Tutorial: Lighting & mood","g":"Tutorials","u":"tutorials/lighting-and-mood.html","h":[["0-set-up-a-fair-test","0. Set up a fair test"],["1-bright-afternoon","1. Bright afternoon"],["2-golden-dusk","2. Golden dusk"],["3-moonlit-night","3. Moonlit night"],["4-check-performance","4. Check performance"],["5-clean-up-and-save","5. Clean up and save"],["6-the-same-look-in-python","6. The same look in Python"],["cheat-sheet","Cheat sheet"]],"x":"Tutorial: Lighting & mood Goal: give the same level three very different moods (bright afternoon, golden dusk, and moonlit night) using only Oberon's Graphics tab. Then reproduce one of them in plain Python. You'll learn: the sun's angles hemisphere ambient tone mapping and exposure fog using test scenes to judge a look fairly Start from: any scene with some objects, such as the coin collector. 0. Set up a fair test # Open the Graphics tab and, under Test Scenes , click Color chart and Shadows . The chart shows how every hue reacts to your settings. The pillars show shadow direction and contrast. They're named Test … , so you'll remove them all at the end with one click. Under Renderer , set Quality to High and note the frame time. 1. Bright afternoon # Environment → Customize Environment , then the Daylight preset. Sun: Elevation 55 : high sun, short shadows, clear shapes. Azimuth 35 : slightly from the side, so cubes show two different shades. Color #FFF5E0 : barely warm. Ambient 0.3 : shadows stay clearly darker than lit ground. Environment: Tone Mapping : ACES. Exposure : 1.1 . Look at the colour chart: saturated reds and blues stay rich, and highlights roll off instead of clipping. Fog on, Fog Density 0.01 , Fog Start 15 . Distant objects pick up a gentle haze, which adds depth. 2. Golden dusk # Click the Dusk preset. Sun: Elevation 8 : long dramatic shadows. Azimuth : rotate until shadows stretch across the play area toward the camera. With the camera behind the player that's about 200 , a low sun from the far side of the level. Color #FFB36B : deep orange. Ambient 0.3 . Notice the ambient: the Sky is violet and the Ground a dark red. Faces pointing up get the cool sky, and the undersides get warm bounce. That colour contrast is most of what makes a scene feel like sunset. Tone Mapping is AgX. Compare it with ACES on the colour chart: AgX keeps the bright orange from turning neon yellow. Click Fog depth under Test Scenes. Raise Fog Density until the farthest pillars just disappear (about 0.03 ), and set Fog Start so the play area stays crisp. 3. Moonlit night # Click the Night preset. Sun (it's the moon now): Elevation 40 , Azimuth 320 . Color #9DB4FF : cold blue. Ambient 0.2 . Night scenes fail when everything is just dark. Keep contrast : The moon stays fairly bright, so lit faces read. Ambient stays low, so shadows are deep. Fog is black and close ( Fog Start 2 ), so the world falls away into darkness. To make it moodier, lower Exposure to 0.8 . Don't lower the moon's colour, which loses the shape of objects. 4. Check performance # Click 400 cubes . Switch Quality between Low, Medium, High, and Ultra and watch the frame time in Renderer . The shadow map is the main cost. Medium is usually indistinguishable from High at a distance. 5. Clean up and save # Under Test Scenes, click Remove N test objects , then Ctrl+S . The look is stored in the scene's Environment and Directional Light entities. Select them in the Hierarchy and the Inspector shows the same fields. To keep all three looks, use File → Save As… after each one, for example level.ron , level_dusk.ron and level_night.ron . Finished files: the example project ships all three: level.ron (afternoon), level_dusk.ron and level_night.ron . Open any of them in Oberon and look at the Graphics tab. 6. The same look in Python # Everything above is data, so code-first games get the same result: Copy Or start from a preset with at.Environment.preset(\"night\") . Environment values are read-only, so to change a few fields of a preset, copy it into a new one. This is how build_level.py makes the three looks: Copy Direction from angles: the light travels toward -(cos(el)·sin(az), sin(el), cos(el)·cos(az)) , where az is Azimuth and el is Elevation in radians. Cheat sheet # Want Change More drama Lower Elevation; lower Ambient Softer, friendlier Higher Ambient; Sky and Ground closer together Depth Fog with Fog Color = Background Punchy colour ACES Bright colours without neon AgX Everything too dark after tone mapping Raise Exposure, not the sun Flat-looking cubes Azimuth off-axis, so neighbouring faces differ Next: From Oberon to Python ."},{"t":"Tutorial: From Oberon to Python","g":"Tutorials","u":"tutorials/oberon-to-python.html","h":[["1-install-the-engine-package","1. Install the engine package"],["2-run-the-scene","2. Run the scene"],["3-whats-different-from-the-editor","3. What's different from the editor"],["4-test-it-headlessly","4. Test it headlessly"],["5-share-it","5. Share it"],["what-you-learned","What you learned"]],"x":"Tutorial: From Oberon to Python Goal: run your Oberon game outside the editor, test it automatically, and package it to share. You'll learn: at.run_scene headless runs writing pytest tests for gameplay the differences between the editor's Play mode and the engine 1. Install the engine package # From the repository root: Copy 2. Run the scene # Create my_game/main.py next to level.ron : Copy Copy run_scene does the following: loads the scene file finds every entity with a script and resolves the path relative to the scene imports the scripts calls start and update every frame, exactly like Oberon's Play mode The camera, physics, lighting, Environment, and attributes all come from the file. The built-in Player controller is an editor convenience. In the engine, characters move only when a script sets entity.character_velocity . Attach player.py to your player so it behaves the same everywhere. 3. What's different from the editor # Oberon Play at.run_scene Scripts Separate Python process, hot reload In-process, no reload A script error Pauses that script and logs it Raises and stops the game, so you see it immediately Everything in Scripting : entity , input, raycast , audio, HUD, cameras ✓ ✓ When writes apply After all scripts have run The same ctx.events , ctx.resources , ctx.quit() — ✓ The rest of the engine's physics (impulses, queued casts) — ✓ Spawning: ctx.raw.scene.spawn(prefab) — ✓ Both columns run the same code for entity and ctx , and a shared test suite holds them to the same results. Stick to the common rows and a script runs identically in both. 4. Test it headlessly # headless=True runs the real engine without a window or GPU: the same physics, the same scripts, as fast as possible. That makes gameplay testable in CI. tests/test_level.py : Copy Copy update= runs after your scripts every frame and receives the raw engine context, so it's the place for assertions. Headless time is real time. A headless run goes as fast as it can, and ctx.time follows the real clock, so 120 frames last a fraction of a second. Anything timed, like the five-second intro, won't have happened yet. Test timed logic directly instead, as below. Testing logic that needs time or input: keep the logic in plain functions and test those directly. Behaviour scripts are ordinary modules: Copy To test update itself, pass small fake objects for entity and ctx that have just the attributes the script uses, such as ctx.time.elapsed , ctx.input.just_pressed and entity.camera . The finished project does this to check that Space skips the intro, that Q / E orbit, and that sprinting widens the view. Finished files: the example project has the game launcher main.py (with a --headless flag and a choice of dusk or night ) and the full test suite tests/test_level.py , which CI runs on every push: Copy 5. Share it # A game is a folder: main.py , level.ron , scripts/ , and any prefabs. To share it: With Python users: zip the folder. They run `pip install automata-engine (the wheel from scripts/build.py ) and then python main.py`. As one download: use a Python bundler such as PyInstaller or Briefcase. Include the scene and scripts as data files. The engine is a normal compiled extension module, so bundlers pick it up automatically. What you learned # at.run_scene plays an Oberon scene with its scripts in the real engine. Characters need a script outside the editor. headless=True with max_frames and update= turns gameplay into unit tests. That's the whole loop: build in Oberon → script in Python → test headlessly → ship."},{"t":"Tutorial: a first-person shooter","g":"Tutorials","u":"tutorials/fps.html","h":[["1-the-assets","1. The assets"],["fix-the-facing-once","Fix the facing once"],["2-the-level","2. The level"],["3-the-player","3. The player"],["mouse-look","Mouse look"],["walking-jumping-and-footsteps","Walking, jumping and footsteps"],["shooting","Shooting"],["the-hud","The HUD"],["4-the-drones","4. The drones"],["5-play","5. Play"],["6-test-it-headless","6. Test it headless"],["7-export-it","7. Export it"],["where-next","Where next"]],"x":"Tutorial: a first-person shooter Time: 40 minutes · You'll need: the first scene and coin collector tutorials, or some time in Oberon You'll build Blaster Arena , a small first-person shooter on floating grass platforms, using Kenney's free (CC0) Starter Kit FPS models and sounds. You look around with the mouse and walk with WASD. You can double-jump, shoot two kinds of blaster, and fight four drones that shoot back. Along the way you'll use: the Assets tab and per-model import settings , Audio Sources and one-shot sounds, mouse look with a captured cursor, ray casts for shooting and line of sight, a HUD drawn from a script, headless tests, and exporting to Windows and Linux. The finished project is in examples/oberon/fps/ . Open it any time to compare: Copy 1. The assets # Make a project folder and copy in Kenney's files, keeping the folders: Copy You can take them from examples/oberon/fps/ or from Kenney's Starter Kit FPS . All the models share one texture atlas, colormap.png . Open Oberon in the folder, then choose Window → Assets . The Assets tab next to the Console shows every model as a thumbnail. Click Sounds to see the sound effects as waveforms, and double-click one to hear it. Fix the facing once # glTF models face +Z, but AUTOMATA's forward is −Z. That's the way a camera looks and the way scripts aim. Kenney's blasters and drone point at +Z, so held as-is they'd point back at you. You could rotate every entity 180°, but scripts set these rotations every frame. It's simpler to fix the files themselves: Drag blaster.glb from the Assets tab into the scene and select it. In the Inspector, find the Import block under the Mesh Renderer. Press +90° next to Rotation twice, so it reads 0, 180, 0 . Do the same for blaster-repeater.glb and enemy-flying.glb . Each file now has a *.glb.import.ron next to it. Every entity that uses the model is turned, in Play, under run_scene and in exported games alike. Delete the test blaster afterwards. See Models → Import settings for scale and pivot. 2. The level # Drag platforms and walls from the Assets tab onto the scene. The example uses Kenney's layout: five platform-large-grass tiles at different heights, four small platform s, two wall-high and two wall-low , plus grass tufts and clouds for decoration. Positions are in build_level.py if you'd like to match them exactly. For each platform and wall, press Add Collider in the Mesh Renderer. Oberon looks at each model. platform-large-grass is nearly a slab, so it gets a box raised to sit on the model's base. The small platforms and the walls have bevels and notches, so they get a Mesh collider made of their own triangles. Either works for static scenery. The example sets every platform's Shape to Mesh , so the bevelled edges are exact, and gives the walls boxes. Leave out the rigid body: a collider with no rigid body is static scenery that never moves. Add a Sun and an Environment from GameObject . In the Graphics tab, set Background and Fog Color to the same blue-grey, (0.36, 0.39, 0.46) , and turn Fog on with a density of about 0.015 , so the far clouds fade into the sky. 3. The player # Create an empty entity named exactly Player at (0, 1, 0) . The drones look for that name. Give it: Component Settings Rigidbody Body Type Kinematic (position) Collider Capsule , Radius 0.3 , Half Height 0.5 Character Controller Defaults (the script does the moving) Camera Field of View 80 Audio Source Clip sounds/walking.ogg , Volume 0.35 , Loop on, Play On Start off Attributes health (Int) = 100 Then add the two weapons as plain entities named Blaster and Blaster Repeater , using their models with Scale 0.4 . Don't give them colliders, or your own shots would hit them. The player script carries them around. Attach scripts/player.py to the Player ( Add Component → Script… , then type the new name). The rest of this section walks through it. The finished file is in the example folder. Mouse look # start captures the cursor, and update turns the mouse movement into yaw (left/right) and pitch (up/down). Esc gives the cursor back, and a click captures it again: Copy The camera then rides at eye height and looks along the view direction: Copy Walking, jumping and footsteps # WASD (or the arrows) moves relative to where you face. Speed eases in rather than snapping, and gravity pulls the controller down unless it's grounded: Copy JUMPS = 2 gives one jump from the ground and one in the air. Each jump plays one of three jump sounds at random, so repeats don't sound mechanical. Footsteps use the Player's own Audio Source. It's a loop, so the script starts it when you begin walking and stops it when you stop: Copy Shooting # A shot is a ray cast from the eye. Whatever it hits first, if that entity has a health attribute, loses some. The Blaster fires three slightly spread rays (a small shotgun). The Repeater fires one ray, but faster. Every hit throws sparks: Copy ignore=entity keeps the ray from hitting your own capsule. ctx.particles.burst plays a one-off effect that cleans up after itself. Here it's the sparks preset, aimed along the hit's normal so the sparks fly back off the wall. A colour above 1 glows. See Particles . The WEAPONS list at the top of the file holds each blaster's cooldown, damage, spread, sound and crosshair. Press E to switch between them. Try changing the numbers while the game runs; scripts hot-reload when you save. The held blaster is just an entity that the script moves in front of the camera every frame, offset right, down and forward along the view axes. The other blaster is parked far below the level. The HUD # ctx.hud draws over the game for one frame, so the script redraws it every frame. Positions are screen pixels, and shapes are placed by their centre: Copy The rest of draw_hud adds a health bar at the bottom left, a red flash when you're hit, and a pip for each drone still flying at the top right. Text uses the built-in font. size is the line height in pixels, shadow=True adds a soft dro"},{"t":"Zero to Hero: The Complete AUTOMATA Tutorial","g":"Tutorials","u":"zero-to-hero.html","h":[["module-0--the-smallest-game","Module 0 — The smallest game"],["module-1--scenes-entities-components","Module 1 — Scenes, entities, components"],["module-2--cameras-the-art-of-seeing","Module 2 — Cameras: the art of seeing"],["module-3--transforms-move-rotate-scale","Module 3 — Transforms: move, rotate, scale"],["module-4--input-and-action-maps","Module 4 — Input and action maps"],["module-5--physics-bodies-and-colliders","Module 5 — Physics: bodies and colliders"],["module-6--the-character-controller","Module 6 — The character controller"],["module-7--lights-shadows-and-quality","Module 7 — Lights, shadows, and quality"],["module-8--events-and-resources","Module 8 — Events and resources"],["module-9--oop-architecture","Module 9 — OOP architecture"],["module-10--headless-testing","Module 10 — Headless testing"],["module-11--capstone-coin-runner","Module 11 — Capstone: Coin Runner"],["where-next","Where next"]],"x":"Zero to Hero: The Complete AUTOMATA Tutorial A twelve-module masterclass. You start with an empty window and end with Coin Runner — a playable 3D platformer with physics, shadows, collectibles, a follow camera, and a headless test suite. Every module is a complete, runnable program. Work through the modules in order; each one adds exactly one concept. Every module and every exercise solution is a file in examples/python/zero_to_hero/ , so you can run any of them ( python examples/python/zero_to_hero/module_06.py ) or check them all at once without a window: Copy Module 0 — The smallest game Module 1 — Scenes, entities, components Module 2 — Cameras: the art of seeing Module 3 — Transforms: move, rotate, scale Module 4 — Input and action maps Module 5 — Physics: bodies and colliders Module 6 — The character controller Module 7 — Lights, shadows, and quality Module 8 — Events and resources Module 9 — OOP architecture Module 10 — Headless testing Module 11 — Capstone: Coin Runner Module 0 — The smallest game # Concept: a Game owns the engine; run drives the loop. Copy Run it. A window opens and stays open until you close it. That is the whole engine contract: Create a Game (options like title , resolution , clear_color , graphics live here). Call run(update) — your update(ctx) callback runs once per frame . The loop ends when the window closes, ctx.quit() is called, or max_frames is reached. run returns the frame count. A game runs once . After run returns, the engine has shut down; build a new Game to run again. Exercise: pass max_frames=120 to run and print the return value. Solution ( exercises/exercise_00.py ): Copy It prints ran 120 frames : the loop stopped itself. Module 1 — Scenes, entities, components # Concept: a scene is a bag of entities; an entity is a bag of components; components are plain data. Copy An orange cube. The anatomy: at.Entity(\"cube\") — a game object. The name is how you find it later ( ctx.scene[\"cube\"] ). Names are optional but you want them for anything you'll look up. .add(component) — attaches data. Chainable. The components that matter for a visible 3D thing: Transform (where it is) and MeshRenderer.cube(size, color) (what it looks like). game.load_scene(scene) — materializes the staged scene into the running world, replacing whatever was there. The golden rule of 3D visibility: a scene draws nothing until it has a Camera3D entity and an entity with both Transform and MeshRenderer . Exercise: add a second cube at (2, 0, 0) with a different color. Solution ( exercises/exercise_01.py ): Copy The camera now aims at (1, 0, 0) , halfway between the cubes, so both are in frame. Module 2 — Cameras: the art of seeing # Concept: the first entity with a camera component becomes the frame's camera. Cameras are plain data — and writable per frame. Copy Facts to internalize: Camera3D(position, target, fov) — position in world space, the point it looks at, vertical field of view in degrees (default 60). view.camera_3d = at.Camera3D(...) inside update replaces the camera next frame . This is how follow cameras, orbit cameras, and cutscene cameras all work. Without any camera, meshes render through a fallback camera at (0, 0, 5) looking at the origin — handy for debugging, never ship it. There's also Camera2D(center, half_height) for orthographic 2D views. Exercise: make the camera bob up and down ( 2.0 + math.sin(t) ) instead of orbiting. Solution ( exercises/exercise_02.py ): Copy Module 3 — Transforms: move, rotate, scale # Concept: Transform is position + rotation + scale; writes you make in update are buffered and applied right after the callback. Copy Key ideas: view.transform returns a live handle. Reads see start-of-frame values — except when you've written to that same entity this frame, in which case you read back your own pending write (\"read your writes\"). Either way the world itself updates right after your callback returns, so the next frame sees the new values. Rotation is stored as a quaternion ; reading rotation_euler back converts, so expect float drift ( 0.6999999 , not 0.7 ). Never compare rotations with == — write them, don't round-trip them. rotation_euler is (x, y, z) in radians , applied in XYZ order. scale multiplies the mesh — MeshRenderer.cube(size=1) with scale=(40, 1, 40) is how you make a floor out of a unit cube. ctx.time gives you delta (seconds since last frame), elapsed (seconds since start), and frame (counter starting at 1). Exercise: pulse the cube's color instead of scale… trick question — color lives on MeshRenderer , which is fixed at spawn. Change size , position , or rotation ; material swaps are a spawn-a-new-entity job (for now). The answer is the module itself, with scale changing. Module 4 — Input and action maps # Concept: poll hardware directly for toys; bind named actions for games. Copy The layers: Raw polling — ctx.input.pressed(at.Key.SPACE) , ctx.input.just_pressed(...) , ctx.input.mouse_just_pressed(...) , ctx.input.cursor . Fine for experiments. ActionMap — bind names to controls , query the names. Three facts worth remembering: bind and bind_axis stack : A/D, arrows, and the left stick all drive move_x above. Values sum and clamp to −1..1. Axes are analog in, analog out ( gamepad_axis ), or digital-as-analog ( key_pair gives −1/0/+1). Bindings are mutable at runtime ( rebind , unbind , unbind_axis ) — a remapping screen is ordinary game code. Enums vs strings — at.Key.SPACE and \"space\" are the same value. The enums ( Key , MouseButton , GamepadButton , GamepadAxis ) exist so your editor autocompletes and the type checker catches typos. Prefer them. Exercise: bind jump to both Key.SPACE and GamepadButton.SOUTH , and print \"jump!\" when it's just-pressed. Solution ( exercises/exercise_04.py ): Copy Module 5 — Physics: bodies and colliders # Concept: RigidBody says how a thing moves, Collider3D says what it hits. Physics steps at a fixed 60 Hz regardless of frame rate. Copy Run it and watch the crate drop onto the floor. B"},{"t":"Oberon","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/index.html","h":[["run-it","Run it"],["the-manual","The manual"],["how-oberon-fits-with-the-engine","How Oberon fits with the engine"]],"x":"Oberon Oberon is the AUTOMATA editor. It's a native desktop app written in Rust. You build scenes visually and attach Python behaviour scripts. Then you press Play to run the scene inside the editor. Scenes save as plain .ron files, and the same files run from Python with automata.run_scene . Run it # Copy Oberon needs a GPU that supports Vulkan, Metal, or DX12 to render the viewport. Without one it still runs and draws the scene as wireframes, so editing works anywhere. For Play mode, Oberon starts a Python interpreter ( python3 , or the one you pick in Settings → Python ). That interpreter needs only the standard library. The automata package is required only when you run scenes outside the editor. The manual # Page What it covers The interface Every panel, moving around the scene, gizmos, Play mode Keyboard shortcuts The complete list Scripting Behaviour scripts, the entity / ctx API, hot reload, errors Cameras Scene cameras, the preview, Look Through, driving cameras from Python Graphics The Graphics panel: quality, sun, environment, fog, test scenes The code editor Writing scripts inside Oberon: completion, language servers, find/replace Models Importing glTF and OBJ models, the Assets tab, import settings (scale, rotation, pivot), fitted colliders Audio Sound files, Audio Sources, previews, playing sounds from scripts Prefabs & attributes Reusable objects and custom per-entity data Exporting your game Building standalone Windows and Linux games (Ctrl+Shift+B) New to Oberon? Do the first scene tutorial first. It takes about ten minutes and covers the main ideas. How Oberon fits with the engine # Copy Editing happens in Rust. The scene is a document with undo history, and every edit is one undo step. Play clones the scene and runs physics and the renderer in Oberon. Your scripts run in a separate Python process. Every frame Oberon sends that process the scene state, and the scripts send back their changes. The process is separate on purpose: a script crash can't take down the editor, and you can switch Python environments without restarting. Stop throws the Play copy away. Your edited scene is unchanged. Shipping doesn't need Oberon at all. at.run_scene(\"level.ron\") runs the same scene and scripts in the engine."},{"t":"The interface","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/interface.html","h":[["top-bar","Top bar"],["hierarchy","Hierarchy"],["files","Files"],["file-browser","File browser"],["scene-view","Scene view"],["inspector","Inspector"],["surfaces","Surfaces"],["graphics","Graphics"],["console","Console"],["assets","Assets"],["play-mode","Play mode"],["status-bar","Status bar"]],"x":"The interface Copy All panel borders can be dragged. Window → Reset Layout restores the default sizes. Top bar # Menus : File (scenes, scripts, prefabs), Edit (undo, clipboard, tools), GameObject (create), Component (add to the selection), Window, Help. Play / Pause / Stop sit in the centre. The scene name next to the menus shows a dot when you have unsaved changes. Search (Ctrl+Shift+P) opens the command palette. Type part of any command, entity, or script name and press Enter. ⚙ (Ctrl+,) opens Settings. Hierarchy # Lists every entity in the scene. Each icon shows the entity's main component: cube, sun, camera, horizon (Environment), or empty. Click to select. Double-click (or press F) to frame the entity in the view. + opens the Create menu: Empty, Cube, Sphere, Plane, Directional Light, Point Light, Spot Light, Camera, Environment, Water, Physics Cube, Player. Right-click an entity for Rename, Duplicate, Copy/Paste, Delete, Frame, Save as Prefab, and Attach/Edit Script. Files # The bottom of the left sidebar is a file tree of your project: the folder your scene is saved in. Drag the divider between Hierarchy and Files to share the space, or hide either one from the Window menu. Click a folder to expand or collapse it. The collapse button in the panel's title bar folds everything back up. Double-click a .py file to open it in the code editor, a .ron file to open it as a scene, or a .prefab to place it in the scene. Models ( .glb , .gltf , .obj , shown with a cube icon) can be dragged onto the scene view to place them, or onto the Inspector to give them to the selected entity. Double-click a model to add it where you're looking. See Models . The open scene and the script you're editing are shown in bright text with a dot. Right-click a file for Open, Edit Script, Attach to the selected entity, or Instantiate. Right-click anywhere for New Script… and New Folder . New files go in the selected folder, and the tree opens to show them. + in the title bar does the same as right-clicking empty space. Drag a file onto a folder to move it there (onto empty space to move it to the project root). Scenes that use the file, such as an attached script, a model or a sound, are updated to the new path in one undo step. Right-click a file or folder for Rename… , Duplicate , Copy Path , Copy Relative Path , Show in File Manager , and Move to Trash . Deleting always asks first and goes to the system trash where there is one, so it can be restored. Click in the panel to give it the keyboard: Keys Action ↑ / ↓ Select the previous / next file → / ← Expand / collapse a folder (← on a file goes to its folder) Enter Open the file (scene, script, prefab) or toggle a folder F2 Rename in place (the extension is kept) Ctrl+D Duplicate Del Move to Trash (asks first) Ctrl+Shift+C Copy the file's path File browser # Open, Save As, New Script, Import Model, and the … buttons in Settings all use the same browser, so you rarely need to type a path: Places on the left: the project, its Scripts/Models/Scenes folders, folders you used recently, and your home folder. Breadcrumbs across the top: click any part of the path to go back to it. ← → ↑ go back, forward and up (Alt+← / Alt+→ / Alt+↑). Click a file to select it, double-click to open it. One click on a folder opens it. Click a column header to sort by name, size or date. Type to filter : the name field narrows the list as you type, and Enter takes the highlighted match, so pla + Enter opens player.py . Tab completes the name. Keyboard : ↑/↓, PgUp/PgDn, Home/End move the highlight; Enter opens a folder or chooses a file; Backspace in an empty field goes up a folder; F5 refreshes; Esc closes. + New Folder makes a folder right there and asks for its name. Saving over an existing file asks first. Dropping a file from your desktop onto the browser jumps to it. Each kind of dialog remembers the folder you used last. Hidden files and __pycache__ , target , and node_modules folders are left out. Scene view # This is the 3D viewport, rendered by the engine's own wgpu renderer. To Do Select Click an object Orbit Drag empty space, right-drag, or Alt+drag Pan Middle-drag Zoom Mouse wheel Frame selection F, or double-click in the hierarchy Context menu Right-click without dragging Gizmos. The floating toolbar (top-left) switches between Move (W), Rotate (E), and Scale (R). Drag a coloured axis handle to edit along that axis. Arrow keys nudge the selection across the ground relative to the view, PgUp/PgDn move it up and down, and holding Shift makes the steps finer. The axis widget (top-right) shows the view orientation. Overlays. Lights draw as icons: a sun for the Directional Light, a bulb for a Point Light, a lamp with its beam for a Spot Light (in the light's own colour). A selected Point Light shows its range as three rings, and a selected Spot Light shows its cone out to its range. The fainter inner circle is where the light is still at full strength. Cameras draw their view frustum : a pyramid showing exactly what the camera sees, with a notch marking \"up\". When a camera is selected, a live preview of its view appears in the bottom-right corner. See Cameras . Inspector # Shows the selected entity. Name at the top. Components : Transform, Mesh Renderer, Directional Light, Point Light, Spot Light, Camera, Environment, Rigidbody, Collider, Character Controller, Script. Each section folds open and closed. The ⋮ menu has Reset, Copy/Paste Component, and Remove. Fields : drag the label to scrub a number, or click the field and type. Colour swatches open a colour picker with hex input. Attributes : custom values your scripts read. See Prefabs & attributes . Add Component lists every component the entity doesn't have yet. Every edit is one undo step. Scrubbing a value counts as one step, not one per frame. Surfaces # Below Color the Mesh Renderer has the material rows: Row What it does Surface A preset: Default, Matte, Glossy, Metal, Brushed Metal, Rubber or Glowing (glows in the mes"},{"t":"Scripting in Oberon","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/scripting.html","h":[["attach-a-script","Attach a script"],["the-frame-model","The frame model"],["entity","entity"],["ctx","ctx"],["hot-reload","Hot reload"],["errors","Errors"],["testing-scripts-headless","Testing scripts headless"],["which-python","Which Python?"],["same-scripts-real-engine","Same scripts, real engine"]],"x":"Scripting in Oberon You write gameplay as behaviour scripts . A behaviour script is a Python file with up to three functions, and you attach it to an entity. Oberon calls those functions while the scene plays. Copy All three functions are optional. start(entity, ctx) runs once, on the entity's first Play frame. update(entity, ctx) runs every frame. stop(entity, ctx) runs when you press Stop. Attach a script # Inspector → Add Component → Script… , or right-click an entity → Attach Script… . Pick an existing .py file or type a new name. A new file starts from a template: a small character controller with a cheat-sheet docstring. The script path is saved relative to the scene file . You can move the project folder and the link still works. Several entities can share one script. Each entity gets its own entity handle and its own entity.state . The frame model # Every frame, Oberon sends the scene state to the Python runner. The runner calls every script, and the scripts' changes come back and apply before the frame renders. Two rules follow from this: Reads see the start of the frame , plus every write made earlier in the same frame, by any script. All scripts share one handle per entity. Writes reach the simulation after all scripts have run. Physics, rendering and sound see the frame's final values. If two scripts set the same value, the last one wins. So when four drones each run ctx.scene[\"Player\"].attributes[\"health\"] -= 5 in one frame, the player loses 20 health, not 5. A despawn also lands at the end of the frame: other scripts can still find the entity until then. run_scene follows the same rules, because both runners use the same code for entity and ctx . entity # Member Meaning entity.name , entity.id Name from the hierarchy; stable numeric id entity.transform.position (x, y, z) . Read it, or assign a tuple entity.transform.rotation_euler (x, y, z) in radians (the Inspector shows degrees) entity.transform.scale (x, y, z) entity.transform.translate(d) / .rotate(d) Add a delta to the position / rotation entity.character_velocity Set it to move a Character Controller entity.grounded True when a character is standing on something entity.attributes[\"key\"] Custom values from the Inspector; read and write like a dict entity.camera The entity's camera, or None ( Cameras ) entity.audio.play() / .stop() Start (or restart) / stop the entity's Audio Source ( Audio ) entity.audio.playing Whether the Audio Source was playing at the start of the frame entity.despawn() Remove the entity until Play stops entity.state A dict of your own that persists between frames (not saved) ctx # Member Meaning ctx.time.delta Seconds since the last frame (multiply speeds by it) ctx.time.elapsed , ctx.time.frame Seconds since Play started; frame number, starting at 1 ctx.input.pressed(\"w\") Key held down ctx.input.just_pressed(\"space\") Pressed this frame ctx.input.just_released(\"e\") Released this frame ctx.input.axis(\"a\", \"d\") −1, 0, or 1 from two keys ctx.input.mouse_delta (dx, dy) mouse movement in pixels since last frame; +y is down ctx.input.mouse_pressed(\"left\") Mouse button held ( \"left\" , \"right\" , \"middle\" ); also mouse_just_pressed and mouse_just_released ctx.input.lock_mouse(True) Hide and capture the cursor for mouse look. False frees it, and Stop always frees it ctx.input.mouse_locked Whether the cursor is captured ctx.physics.grounded(entity) Same as entity.grounded ctx.physics.raycast(origin, direction, max_distance=100, ignore=None) The first collider along a ray, or None . Returns a Hit with .entity , .point , .normal and .distance . ignore skips one entity, usually the caster. Sensors are skipped ctx.physics.water_height(x, z) Height of the water surface at a world point, waves included, or None where there's no water ctx.water.height(x, z) The same ctx.water.splash(position, strength=1.0) A splash at a point or an entity: rings spread out and foam gathers. strength runs from 0 to 10; 1 is about a crate dropped from a couple of metres. Things that fall in or move through water make their own ripples ctx.particles.burst(\"sparks\", position, direction=None, count=None, **fields) A one-off particle effect at a point or an entity that cleans up after itself: \"fire\" , \"smoke\" , \"sparks\" , \"dust\" , \"splash\" or \"magic\" , with any field changed ( color=(0.3, 1, 0.4, 1) ). direction aims it, for example along a hit's normal . count is how many particles. See Particles ctx.particles.emit(entity, count) Extra particles from the entity's own Particle Emitter ctx.audio.play(\"sounds/hit.ogg\", volume=1, pitch=1, position=None) Play a sound once. With a position (a point or an entity) it fades with distance ctx.hud.image(\"sprites/crosshair.png\", x, y, size=None, color=(1,1,1,1)) Draw an image this frame, centred on (x, y) in screen pixels ctx.hud.rect(x, y, width, height, color) Draw a filled rectangle this frame, centred on (x, y) ctx.hud.text(\"100%\", x, y, size=24, color=(1,1,1,1), align=\"left\", shadow=None, outline=None) Draw text this frame with the built-in font (see below) ctx.hud.size , ctx.hud.center The game view's size in pixels, and its centre ctx.scene[\"Name\"] Another entity by name (raises KeyError if missing) ctx.scene.find(\"Name\") The same, but returns None if missing ctx.scene.by_id(id) An entity by id (as in hit.entity.id ), or None \"Name\" in ctx.scene Whether an entity has that name for e in ctx.scene: Every entity ctx.camera The camera Play renders through, or None ctx.log(...) , ctx.warn(...) Write to the Console. print() works too HUD shapes last one frame, so draw them in update every frame you want them shown. Image paths are relative to the scene folder. Each call draws on top of the previous one. Text uses the built-in font (Geist Medium): size is the line height in pixels. align says where x sits on each line: \"left\" (its left edge), \"center\" or \"right\" . y is the vertical centre, and \\n starts a new line. shadow and outline take a colour, or True for a translucent black shadow or a solid "},{"t":"Cameras","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/cameras.html","h":[["add-a-camera","Add a camera"],["camera-fields","Camera fields"],["see-what-the-camera-sees","See what the camera sees"],["cameras-in-play-mode","Cameras in Play mode"],["driving-a-camera-from-a-script","Driving a camera from a script"],["recipes","Recipes"],["cameras-in-plain-python","Cameras in plain Python"]],"x":"Cameras AUTOMATA has two kinds of view: The editor view is the free orbit camera you navigate with in Oberon. It isn't part of the scene and is never saved. Scene cameras are entities with a Camera component. They're saved in the scene file. Play mode and the shipped game render through the first one. Add a camera # + → Camera in the Hierarchy, or GameObject → Camera . You can also use Graphics → Camera → Add Camera . You can add a Camera component to any entity too: Add Component → Camera . A new camera is placed where you're currently looking from, aimed at the same point, so it starts with a sensible view. Camera fields # Field Meaning Field of View Vertical angle in degrees. Around 60 is natural; 30 is a telephoto zoom; 90+ is a wide action view Near Clip / Far Clip Nothing closer than Near or farther than Far is drawn. Keep Near as large as you can (0.1–0.5) to avoid depth flicker Eye Where the camera is Look At The point it aims at The camera aims at a point rather than storing a rotation, because that matches how games usually move cameras: follow something, look at something. Moving the entity moves the camera. When you drag a camera with the Move gizmo, or change its Transform position, Eye and Look At move with it. That keeps the aim. Editing Eye or Look At directly doesn't drag the Transform along. See what the camera sees # Frustum. Every camera draws its view pyramid in the scene view. The selected one is brighter, and a small notch marks the top edge. Preview. While a camera is selected, a live render of its view appears in the bottom-right corner of the scene view. You can turn it off with Graphics → Camera → Preview . It hides automatically when the view is too small. Look Through jumps the editor view to the camera's position, aim, and field of view. It's the quickest way to frame a shot exactly. Align to View does the reverse: it moves the camera to where the editor view is now. A good workflow is to navigate until the shot looks right, then click Align to View. It's one undo step. Look Through and Align to View are in the Inspector's Camera section and in Graphics → Camera . Cameras in Play mode # When the scene has a camera, Play renders through the first one in the hierarchy. The editor's own view is ignored until you Stop. The built-in player's WASD movement is relative to the camera's facing. With no camera, Play uses the editor view and follows the player. That's handy for quick tests, but a shipped game should always have a camera. Driving a camera from a script # Attach a script to the camera entity, or to anything else, and use entity.camera or ctx.camera : Copy Member Meaning cam.position Eye (x, y, z) . Read it or assign it cam.target Look-at point (x, y, z) cam.fov Vertical field of view in degrees (clamped to 1–179) cam.look_at(point_or_entity) Aim at a point or at an entity's position cam.follow(entity, offset=(0, 3, 8), smooth=8.0, look_height=0.6) Ease toward entity + offset and look at the entity. Call it every frame. smooth=0 snaps The same API works in Oberon's Play mode and under at.run_scene . Recipes # Fixed security camera that tracks the player Copy Side-scroller : lock the camera's Z distance and follow only X and Y. Copy Orbit with Q / E Copy Zoom while a key is held (Z) Copy More: the third-person camera tutorial and the camera rail / cutscene tutorial . Cameras in plain Python # In code-first games (no Oberon), cameras are ordinary components. See Guide: Cameras ."},{"t":"Graphics","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/graphics.html","h":[["renderer","Renderer"],["point-and-spot-lights","Point and spot lights"],["sun","Sun"],["environment","Environment"],["camera","Camera"],["test-scenes","Test Scenes"],["a-tuning-workflow","A tuning workflow"]],"x":"Graphics The Graphics tab sits next to the Inspector in the right dock. It collects everything that decides how the frame looks, plus tools for testing it. Every edit is undoable and saved with the scene, except Quality and Preview , which are editor preferences. The panel edits real scene entities. Each group shows which entity it edits, for example On entity \"Sun\" , with a Select button that jumps to that entity in the Inspector. Nothing in the panel is hidden state: a scene tuned here looks the same in Play and under at.run_scene . Renderer # Quality : Low / Medium / High / Ultra / Beautiful. This sets the shadow map resolution, or turns shadows off on Low. Beautiful switches to contact-hardening cascaded shadows, ambient occlusion, physically based light, HDR bloom, FXAA and refractive water. It applies immediately. Stats update live: frame time in ms, fps, meshes drawn, and shadow map size. Use them with the 400 cubes test scene to see what your scene costs. Quality Shadows Shadow maps Glow lights with shadows Low off — none Medium on 1 × 1024 px none High (default) crisp, 2 cascades 2 × 2048 px 2 nearest Ultra crisp, 3 cascades 3 × 2048 px 4 nearest Beautiful contact-hardening, 4 cascades, plus ambient occlusion 4 × 2048 px 4 nearest, soft Water is a component. Use Create → Water (it starts as a calm pond) or select an entity and use Add Component → Water in the Inspector. It has fields for size, colors, clarity, waves and foam. Tick Endless on a sea to carry it, calm, past its edges to the horizon. See Rendering & Lights . Point and spot lights # Use Create → Point Light for a lamp or torch, and Create → Spot Light for a flashlight, headlight or stage light. You can also add either to any entity with Add Component . A point light shines every way from the entity. A spot light shines along the entity's forward axis (its blue Z handle points backward), so aim it with the Rotate tool. New spot lights start tipped down, lighting the ground in front of them. Field What it does Color The light's colour Intensity Brightness. 1 lights a surface 1 m away like a white sun overhead. Torches and lamps 2–6, headlights 10–30 Range Where the light ends (m). 0 works it out from the brightness Cone Angle (Spot) the cone's edge, in degrees from its axis (up to 80) Inner Angle (Spot) full strength inside this angle. Closer to the cone angle gives a harder edge Radius The size of the glowing source. Bigger looks softer up close Cast Shadows Whether objects block it (High and up; see the shadow table) Placed lights share the shadowed-light budget in the table above with glowing meshes. A spot light costs a sixth of a point light, so a pair of headlights is cheap. A small mesh around a light (a bulb, a flame, a lens) never blocks it. See Rendering & Lights . Sun # This group edits the scene's first Directional Light . If there isn't one, Add Directional Light creates it. Field Meaning Time of Day Moves the sun along a simple day arc in one drag: 6 = sunrise, 12 = highest (65°), 18 = sunset. Sets Azimuth and Elevation together Azimuth Compass direction the light comes from , in degrees. 0 = from +Z (behind the default view), 90 = from +X Elevation Height of the sun above the horizon. 90 = straight down (noon), 5–15 = long sunset shadows Color Sunlight tint Ambient How bright the shadowed sides are (0 = black shadows, 1 = no shading) Cast Shadows Shadow mapping for this light Shadow Distance How far from the camera shadows reach, in metres (default 120). They fade out beyond it. Shorter is sharper Shadow Softness How soft shadow edges are: 0 sharpest, 1 default, up to 8. Shows most on Beautiful, where shadows start sharp at the object and widen with distance by the Sun Size times this Azimuth and elevation are a friendlier way to set the light's direction vector. The Inspector's Directional Light section shows the same two fields. Environment # The Environment component sets the look of the whole scene: the sky and sun, ambient colour, exposure, tone mapping, and fog. The first entity that has one wins. A scene without one uses the neutral default look: white ambient, a near-black background, no fog, and no tone mapping. Click Customize Environment to create an Environment entity and start tuning. Presets give you a starting point: Preset Look Tone mapping Neutral Plain lighting, no fog: the default None Daylight Blue sky with sun and clouds, warm ground bounce, light haze ACES Dusk Violet sky with a big warm sun, dark red ground, dense warm fog AgX Night Near-black sky with a small dim disk, cold ambient, black fog ACES Field Meaning Sky / Ground Ambient light from above and from below. Surfaces facing up take the sky colour; surfaces facing down take the ground colour; the rest blend between them. This hemisphere ambient makes flat-coloured scenes look grounded Sky & Sun Draw a sky dome instead of a flat colour: a gradient from Horizon to Zenith, the sun disk where the Directional Light shines from, a halo around it, and drifting clouds. Water and fog pick up the same sky Background / Horizon With the sky off, the flat colour behind everything. With it on, the sky colour at the horizon (also the far fog's colour) Zenith Sky colour straight up Sun Size Diameter of the sun disk in degrees (the real sun is about 0.5; 2 reads well in games) Sun Glow Brightness of the halo around the sun. Low suns glow warmer on their own Clouds Cloud cover, 0 = clear, 1 = overcast Lens Flare Strength of the glare, starburst and ghosts when you look toward the sun. Beautiful quality only; scenery and clouds in front of the sun hide it Tone Mapping How bright values are squeezed into the screen's range. None : values above 1 clip. Reinhard : soft and flat. ACES : filmic contrast and saturation. AgX : filmic, keeps bright colours from turning neon Exposure Brightness multiplier applied before tone mapping. Raise it with ACES or AgX, which darken mid-tones Occlusion Ambient occlusion strength: how much creases, corners and the"},{"t":"The code editor","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/code-editor.html","h":[["open-a-script","Open a script"],["tabs","Tabs"],["editing","Editing"],["completion","Completion"],["language-servers","Language servers"],["settings","Settings"]],"x":"The code editor Oberon has a native code editor built in, so you can write scripts without leaving the scene. Scripts open as tabs next to the Scene tab. Press Esc to go back to the scene view; the tab stays open. Open a script # Double-click a .py file in Files (left sidebar). File → New Script… / Open Script… . Right-click an entity → Edit Script . Click a file:line link in the Console. Ctrl+E (Go to File): type part of a script name. Open scripts come first, most recent first. The command palette (Ctrl+Shift+P) finds scripts too. Tabs # The keys are the same as in VS Code, and they also work while the Scene tab is showing: Ctrl+Tab shows your open tabs in the order you last used them. Keep Ctrl held and press Tab again to go further back; let go to switch. Ctrl+PgUp / Ctrl+PgDn steps through the tabs in strip order. Alt+1…9 jumps to a tab by position (the Scene is Alt+1). Ctrl+W closes a tab, and Ctrl+Shift+T brings back the last one you closed. When you close the active tab, Oberon shows the tab you used before it. Middle-click a tab to close it, or drag it to reorder. Right-click a tab for Close Others , Close to the Right , Close Saved , Copy Path and Reveal in Files . When there are too many tabs to fit, scroll the strip with the wheel. Editing # You get what you'd expect from a modern editor: Python syntax highlighting auto-indent after : bracket and quote pairing line moves, duplicates, and comments indentation guides current-line highlight multi-line paste that keeps relative indentation multiple carets: Ctrl+D adds the next match of the selected word, Ctrl+Shift+L selects every match, Alt+click adds a caret, and Ctrl+Alt+↑/↓ adds one above or below. Every caret types at once, and one Ctrl+Z undoes the whole edit. The full key list is in Keyboard shortcuts . Find / Replace (Ctrl+F / Ctrl+H) supports case-sensitive and whole-word matching, shows match counts, and can replace all at once. The status line shows the cursor position, selection size, diagnostics counts, indentation, encoding, and line endings. Completion # Completion is built in and needs no setup. It knows the scripting API: ctx. → time , input , scene , camera , physics , log , … entity.transform. → position , rotation_euler , scale , … ctx.camera. / entity.camera. → position , target , fov , look_at , follow inside ctx.input.pressed(\" : every key name inside ctx.scene[\" : the names of the entities in your scene inside entity.attributes[\" : the attribute names you've defined Each suggestion has an icon for its kind: a cube for functions and methods, [▪] for variables, linked boxes for classes, a tag for properties, { } for modules, a boxed = for constants, and lines for keywords. Suggestions appear as you type. Turn that off in Settings → Script Editor if you'd rather press Ctrl+Space . Hover a name for its documentation. Language servers # If a Python language server is installed, Oberon starts it when you open a script. It adds: diagnostics (squiggles plus the status-line counts) richer completion hover F12 go to definition Oberon looks for these, in order: basedpyright , pyright , pylsp , jedi-language-server . Copy Settings → Python → Language server shows which one is running and has a Restart server button. Settings # Settings → Script Editor has these options: font size Editor scale : zooms only the code editor (text, gutter, find bar, popups and status line) and leaves the rest of the interface alone. You can also hold Ctrl and use the wheel over the editor. The whole-interface zoom is Settings → Appearance → Interface scale . current-line highlight indentation guides whitespace markers suggest while typing trim trailing whitespace on save Save scripts before Play , on by default, so Play always runs what you see Prefer another editor? Edit the files in VS Code, Vim, or anything else. Oberon hot-reloads saved scripts during Play either way."},{"t":"Models","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/models.html","h":[["animated-models","Animated models"],["add-a-model-to-the-scene","Add a model to the scene"],["the-assets-tab","The Assets tab"],["what-importing-copies","What importing copies"],["give-a-model-to-an-existing-entity","Give a model to an existing entity"],["the-inspector","The Inspector"],["import-settings","Import settings"],["colliders","Colliders"],["editing-a-model-while-oberon-is-open","Editing a model while Oberon is open"],["scene-files-and-paths","Scene files and paths"],["from-python","From Python"],["try-it","Try it"],["troubleshooting","Troubleshooting"]],"x":"Models Import 3D models made in Blender or any other modelling tool, or downloaded from an asset store, and give them to your entities. Oberon reads two formats: Format Files Notes glTF 2.0 .glb (one file) or .gltf (+ .bin , images) Recommended. Blender: File → Export → glTF 2.0 . Wavefront OBJ .obj (+ .mtl , images) Universal and simple. Has no node hierarchy. From each glTF material Oberon takes the full PBR set: base colour and its texture, roughness and metallic (factors and the metallic-roughness map), the normal map , and emission (colour, map and KHR_materials_emissive_strength ). Textures can be PNG or JPEG. Spec-gloss materials and KHR_texture_transform are converted too. OBJ files bring base colour and texture, and are drawn as a non-metal with roughness 0.6. The model draws with the engine's lighting, shadows and fog, like the built-in cubes. Animated models # glTF files keep their skeleton and animations : skinned meshes (up to four bones per vertex, 128 bones per model) and parts moved by animated nodes, like a robot whose arms are separate meshes. The renderer poses them on the GPU, and shadows follow the pose. For now an animated model shows its rest pose in the scene view and in your game. Playing clips from scripts ( entity.animation.play(\"run\") ) and previewing them in the Inspector come next. Rust games can already play clips with the Animator component. Add a model to the scene # Pick whichever way suits you: Pick it in the Assets tab (below). Every model in the project is shown as a thumbnail. Drag a file from your system file manager onto the scene view. Oberon copies it into the project's models/ folder and places it where you dropped it, standing on the ground. Drag a model from the Files panel onto the scene view. Double-click a model in the Files panel to add it where the view is looking. GameObject → Model… (or + → Create Model… in the Hierarchy) opens a searchable list of the project's models. Choose Import from file… at the bottom of the list to pick a file anywhere on disk. The new entity is named after the file ( oak_tree.glb becomes Oak tree ). It shows the whole model: every mesh and material in the file draws as one entity, so you move, rotate and scale it as one object. The Assets tab # Window → Assets opens the Assets tab next to the Console. It shows every model in the project as a rendered thumbnail, so you can find the crate you want without reading file names. Control What it does Models / Sounds Switch between model thumbnails and sound waveforms. The count is the number of files. Filter Type part of a name or folder ( walls , enemy ) to narrow the grid. Import… Copy a model or sound from anywhere on disk into the project. Tiles work like files in the Files panel: Drag a model tile onto the scene to place it there, or onto the Inspector to give it to the selected entity. Double-click a tile to add the model where the view is looking. Right-click a tile for the same menu as in the Files panel ( Add to Scene , Use on <entity> , …). Thumbnails render on the CPU, so they work without a GPU too. They're cached, and they redraw when the file or its import settings change. What importing copies # Only the project folder ships with an exported game, so files from outside the project are copied in: A self-contained file ( .glb , or a .gltf / .obj that uses no other files) goes to models/<name> . A model with companion files ( .bin , .mtl , textures) gets its own folder, models/<name>/ . The companions keep their paths relative to the model, so the model's internal references keep working. A model that's already inside the project is used where it is. Give a model to an existing entity # Select the entity and drag a model from the Files panel onto the Inspector . Or open the Mesh Renderer's Primitive menu and choose Model… . Or right-click a model in the Files panel and choose Use on <entity> . If the entity has a collider, it's refitted to the new model (see Colliders ). The Inspector # With a model selected, the Mesh Renderer section shows: Row What it does Primitive Model . Switch to Cube, Sphere or Quad to go back to a primitive. File The model's path relative to the scene. It turns red if the file is missing or can't be read. Double-click it to show the file in the Files panel. summary Parts (one per material), triangle count, size in metres, and whether it's textured. If loading failed, this line shows the error instead. Change… Pick a different model. Add Collider / Fit Collider Adds a collider shaped for the model, or refits the existing one. Tint Multiplies every material's colour. Leave it white to see the model as authored. Surface , Roughness , Metallic , Emission Leave them at Default to keep the file's materials. Setting one overrides it on every part. See Surfaces . Glow Light Glowing parts of the model light their surroundings. This scales that light (0 = off). Glow Shadows Whether other objects block that light and cast shadows from it (High and up). Import settings # Models from different tools and stores rarely agree on size, facing or origin. Instead of fixing every entity that uses a model, fix the model once. Its Import block in the Inspector (under the Mesh Renderer) holds three settings: Setting What it does Quick buttons Scale Multiplies the model's size. Use it for kits authored in centimetres or at toy scale. ×0.01 , ×10 Rotation Turns the model, in degrees about X, Y and Z, before the entity's own rotation. +90° about Y Pivot Where the entity's position sits on the model: File (the file's own origin), Bottom (centre of the base, so it stands on the ground) or Centre . Type a value or use a quick button, and every entity using that file updates at once, along with its thumbnail. Reset goes back to the file as it is. The settings apply wherever the model is used: in Play, under run_scene , and in exported games. Settings are saved next to the model as <file>.import.ron . For example, models/blaster.glb.import.ron : Copy Commit thes"},{"t":"Audio","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/audio.html","h":[["add-a-sound","Add a sound"],["hear-it-before-you-play","Hear it before you play"],["the-audio-source","The Audio Source"],["from-scripts","From scripts"],["from-python","From Python"],["exporting","Exporting"],["troubleshooting","Troubleshooting"]],"x":"Audio Sounds work the same way as models. Put a sound file in the project and drop it into the scene. The entity that's created plays it, and scripts can start and stop it. Format Notes .ogg (Vorbis) Recommended: small files that loop cleanly. .wav Uncompressed. Good for short effects. .mp3 , .flac Also supported. Add a sound # Drag a sound file from your system file manager onto the scene view. Oberon copies it into the project's sounds/ folder and creates an entity with an Audio Source where you dropped it. Drag a sound from the Files panel or the Assets tab onto the scene view, or onto the Inspector to give it to the selected entity. GameObject → Sound… (or + → Create Sound… in the Hierarchy) opens a searchable list of the project's sounds. GameObject → Import Sound… picks a file anywhere on disk. Or select an entity and use Add Component → Audio Source , then pick a clip with Change… . Right-click a sound in the Files panel for Preview , Add to Scene and Play from <entity> (sets the selected entity's clip). Hear it before you play # Double-click a sound in the Files panel or the Assets tab to preview it. In the Assets tab's Sounds view, each sound shows its waveform and length, so you can find the short click among the long loops without listening to all of them. The Audio Source # Row What it does Clip The sound file, relative to the scene, with its length, sample rate and waveform. It turns red if the file is missing. Preview plays it; Change… picks another. Volume 1 plays it as recorded; 0.5 is quieter. Pitch Speed and pitch together: 2 is twice as fast and an octave up. Loop Start over at the end. Use it for music, engines and footsteps. Play On Start Start playing as soon as the scene runs. 3D (fade) Quieter with distance from the camera, and panned left or right. Range With 3D on: the distance in metres at which it falls silent. A looping sound stops when its entity is despawned, and every sound stops when you press Stop. From scripts # Play an entity's Audio Source: Copy entity.audio.playing says whether it's playing. Footsteps are a looping source that you start and stop as the player moves: Copy For one-off effects, you don't need a component. ctx.audio.play plays any file in the project once: Copy A position can be an entity or an (x, y, z) point. Both calls work the same in Oberon's Play mode, under run_scene , and in exported games. From Python # Copy In the scene file it's one more component: Copy Exporting # Sounds are copied with the rest of the project. Build → Build Game stops with an error if an Audio Source points at a missing file, and warns about an Audio Source with no clip. Troubleshooting # Symptom Fix Nothing plays in Oberon Oberon plays sound through your system's default output device. If there's none (or Oberon was built with --no-default-features ), sounds are silent, but scripts and entity.audio.playing still work. A 3D sound is silent The camera is further away than Range . Raise it, or turn 3D off. A loop clicks at the seam The file has silence or a jump at its ends. Trim it in an audio editor, or use .ogg . Clip row red The file moved. Change… to re-pick it."},{"t":"Prefabs & attributes","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/prefabs-and-attributes.html","h":[["attributes","Attributes"],["prefabs","Prefabs"],["from-python","From Python"]],"x":"Prefabs & attributes Attributes # Attributes are named values that you set per entity in the Inspector and read from scripts. Use them to tune gameplay without touching code: a coin's value, an enemy's speed, a door's key colour. Inspector → Attributes → + Add attribute asks for a name and a type: Type Python value Example Bool True / False locked Int int value = 10 Float float speed = 3.5 Text str next_level = \"cave.ron\" Vec3 (x, y, z) tuple patrol_to Color (r, g, b, a) tuple tint In a script, entity.attributes works like a dict: Copy Attributes are saved in the scene file, so they're part of the level data. Writes made during Play are visible to other scripts on the next frame. They're thrown away on Stop, like every other Play change. entity.state is the place for private values your script doesn't want to show in the Inspector. Prefabs # A prefab is an entity saved to its own file ( .prefab ) so you can stamp copies of it into any scene. Save: right-click an entity → Save as Prefab… , or File → Save Selection as Prefab… . The entity's components, attributes, and attached script all go into the file. Use: File → Instantiate Prefab… drops a copy on the ground at the point the view is looking at. Prefab files are plain RON scene snippets. You can diff them, review them, and generate them. From Python # Copy Spawning from Oberon behaviour scripts isn't supported in the editor's Play mode yet. Place prefabs in the scene and enable or move them from scripts instead, or run the scene with at.run_scene , where the full engine API is available through ctx.raw ."},{"t":"Exporting your game","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/exporting.html","h":[["build-from-oberon","Build from Oberon"],["export-templates","Export templates"],["build-from-the-command-line","Build from the command line"],["running-an-exported-game","Running an exported game"],["how-it-works","How it works"]],"x":"Exporting your game An export turns your project into a game other people can run. They don't need Oberon, Rust or Python installed. AUTOMATA exports to Windows and Linux (64-bit). Android is planned. Each export is a folder you can zip and share. You can also turn on Archive to get a ready-made .zip (Windows) or .tar.gz (Linux). Copy Build from Oberon # Save your scene into the project folder (Ctrl+S). Open Build ▸ Build Settings… . Set the game name and version, and pick the platforms. Press Build now , or Ctrl+Shift+B anywhere in the editor. The build runs in the background, so you can keep working while it runs. Its progress appears in the status bar and the Console. If anything fails, the Console opens and lists every problem: a missing script, a scene that won't load, or a missing template. Open build folder shows the result in your file manager. Setting What it does Game name Window title, executable name and folder name Version Used in archive names, e.g. Coin Run-0.1.0-linux-x86_64.zip Start scene The scene the game opens. If empty, the build uses the scene open in Oberon. Output folder Where builds go, relative to the project ( build ) Platforms What Build / Ctrl+Shift+B exports. Build ▸ Build for Linux / Windows exports just one. Archive Also write a .zip / .tar.gz of each build Settings are saved to build.ron in the project folder, so commit it with your project. Each build leaves out .oberon/ , __pycache__/ , version control folders, virtual environments, the output folder and tests/ . To leave out more, add paths to exclude in build.ron . A trailing / means a folder: Copy Export templates # An export doesn't compile anything. It copies a prebuilt export template (the game launcher plus a private Python with the engine installed), then adds your project. That's why a build takes seconds, and why you can export for Windows from Linux and the other way round. Templates are downloaded once per engine version. Build fetches a missing template by itself, or you can use Build ▸ Download Export Templates or the Download buttons on the Build page. The Linux template is about 75 MB and the Windows one about 25 MB. While the repository is private, the plain download link needs you to be signed in. Oberon and automata-export try these in order: the public release link; the GitHub CLI, if you've signed in with gh auth login ; a token in GH_TOKEN or GITHUB_TOKEN (it needs read access to the repository). If none of them works and you run Oberon from a source checkout ( cargo run -p oberon ), Build compiles automata-player with cargo and uses it as a Development template for your own platform. The Build page shows each template's state: Ready : the game ships its own Python. This is what you give to players. Development : the game uses the Python on PATH , which must have automata installed. Oberon sets one up automatically when you run a source checkout and no template is installed yet. It's fine for testing on your own machine, but don't ship it. Not installed : Build downloads it the first time you build for that platform. Templates live in ~/.local/share/automata/templates/<version>/ on Linux and %APPDATA%\\automata\\templates\\<version>\\ on Windows. You can choose another folder by setting AUTOMATA_TEMPLATES . Build from the command line # The same exporter is available as automata-export . Use it in CI, or for code-first games that don't use Oberon: Copy A code-first game, written in Python with at.Game and no scene file, starts from a script instead of a scene: Copy The exported game runs main.py exactly like python main.py would, with the game folder as the working directory. Load files with paths relative to that folder. Running an exported game # Players start the executable. It also accepts two flags, which help with smoke tests in CI: Flag Effect --headless Run without a window --frames N Quit after N frames Copy Each run writes its output and any Python traceback to logs/last_run.log next to the executable. On Windows, the game has no console window, so when it crashes the log opens in Notepad. How it works # automata-player is a small native launcher written in Rust. It finds runtime/ and starts its Python with python -m automata.player game/ . The player reads game/game.json , which holds the name, the start scene or main script, and the resolution, then runs the game. Scenes run through at.run_scene and scripts through runpy . Templates come from the Export templates workflow on GitHub. It builds the launcher and the engine wheel on Windows and Linux, installs them into a portable CPython 3.12, smoke-tests an export of Coin Run , and publishes the results to the export-templates release."},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","g":"Oberon","u":"oberon/shortcuts.html","h":[["everywhere","Everywhere"],["scene","Scene"],["file-browser","File browser"],["files-panel-click-it-first","Files panel (click it first)"],["play-mode-default-player","Play mode (default player)"],["code-editor","Code editor"],["multiple-carets","Multiple carets"],["tabs","Tabs"]],"x":"Keyboard shortcuts Press F1 inside Oberon to see this list in the app. On macOS, Ctrl means ⌘. Everywhere # Keys Action Ctrl+N New scene Ctrl+O Open scene Ctrl+S Save scene (or the focused script) Ctrl+Shift+S Save scene as Ctrl+P Play / Stop Ctrl+Shift+P Command palette Ctrl+E Go to file (scripts by name, open ones first) Ctrl+Shift+B Build the game ( Exporting ) Ctrl+, Settings Ctrl+= / Ctrl+− / Ctrl+0 Interface zoom in / out / reset Ctrl+wheel over the code editor Editor zoom only (Settings › Script Editor › Editor scale) Ctrl+Q Quit F1 Keyboard shortcuts Scene # Keys Action Click Select Drag, right-drag, Alt+drag Orbit Middle-drag Pan Wheel Zoom F Frame the selection W / E / R Move / Rotate / Scale gizmo Arrows Nudge the selection across the ground, relative to the view PgUp / PgDn Nudge the selection up / down Shift + nudge Fine steps Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y Undo / Redo Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V Copy / Paste entity Ctrl+D Duplicate Del Delete F2 Rename Esc Deselect File browser # Keys Action Type Filter the list ↑ / ↓, PgUp / PgDn, Home / End Move the highlight Enter Open the folder / choose the file Tab Complete the name Backspace (empty name) or Alt+↑ Up one folder Alt+← / Alt+→ Back / Forward F5 Refresh Esc Close Files panel (click it first) # Keys Action ↑ / ↓ Select the previous / next file → / ← Expand / collapse Enter Open F2 Rename Ctrl+D Duplicate Del Move to Trash Ctrl+Shift+C Copy path Play mode (default player) # Keys Action W A S D or arrows Move (relative to the camera) Space Jump Shift Sprint Your scripts can read any key: ctx.input.pressed(\"q\") . Code editor # Keys Action Ctrl+Space Complete F12 Go to definition Ctrl+F / Ctrl+H Find / Replace Ctrl+G Go to line Ctrl+/ Toggle comment Ctrl+K Ctrl+C / Ctrl+K Ctrl+U Comment / uncomment Ctrl+Shift+K Delete line Alt+↑ / Alt+↓ Move line up / down Shift+Alt+↑ / Shift+Alt+↓ Copy line up / down Ctrl+] / Ctrl+[ Indent / outdent Ctrl+L Select line Ctrl+Enter / Ctrl+Shift+Enter New line below / above Ctrl+Shift+Backslash Jump to the matching bracket Ctrl+↑ / Ctrl+↓ Scroll without moving the caret Esc Drop extra carets, then the selection, then leave the editor Multiple carets # Every caret types, deletes, pastes and completes at once, and one Ctrl+Z undoes the whole edit. Keys Action Ctrl+D Select the word, then add the next match Ctrl+Shift+L Select every match Alt+click Add or remove a caret Ctrl+Alt+↑ / Ctrl+Alt+↓ Add a caret above / below Ctrl+C / Ctrl+X / Ctrl+V Copy / cut every selection; pasting as many lines as carets puts one line at each Tabs # These work from the Scene tab too. The Scene is always the first tab. Keys Action Ctrl+Tab / Ctrl+Shift+Tab Recently used tabs. Hold Ctrl and press Tab again to go further back; release Ctrl to switch Ctrl+PgDn / Ctrl+PgUp Next / previous tab Alt+1 … Alt+9 Go to tab by position (Alt+1 is the Scene) Alt+0 Last tab Ctrl+Shift+PgDn / Ctrl+Shift+PgUp Move the tab right / left Ctrl+W / Ctrl+F4 Close the tab Ctrl+Shift+T Reopen the last closed tab Ctrl+K W / Ctrl+K U Close all / close saved tabs (unsaved tabs stay open) Ctrl+K S Save all With the mouse: middle-click a tab to close it, drag it to reorder it, and right-click it for Close Others, Close to the Right, Copy Path and Reveal in Files. The wheel scrolls the tab strip when tabs overflow."},{"t":"Guide: The Game Loop","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/game-loop.html","h":[["the-loop","The loop"],["the-frame-contract","The frame contract"],["ending-the-loop","Ending the loop"],["frame-pacing","Frame pacing"],["headless-mode","Headless mode"],["errors","Errors"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: The Game Loop The loop is the engine's heartbeat. This guide is the mental model the rest of the wiki assumes. The loop # Copy Every frame, in order: Time advances — ctx.time.delta (seconds since last frame), elapsed , frame (1-based counter) are refreshed. Events age — an event sent this frame becomes readable next frame, then expires. Physics steps — a fixed 60 Hz timestep, as many steps as fit the elapsed frame time. Frame rate never changes simulation speed. Your update(ctx) runs — input snapshot, scene snapshot, buffered writes. Your buffered writes apply — transforms, velocities, camera changes, spawns, despawns all land at once. The frame renders (windowed mode) — the scene is recorded into a draw list and presented. The frame contract # Two rules prevent an entire category of engine bugs: Reads observe start-of-frame state. ctx.scene , ctx.input , ctx.physics are snapshots taken as the frame began. If physics moves the player mid-frame and you write a camera from player.transform , you see this frame's settled position — consistent, never half-updated. (Reading an entity you just wrote to returns your pending write.) Writes apply after your callback returns. `view.transform.position = ... , view.despawn() , ctx.scene.spawn(...)` are commands in a queue, not live mutation. They become visible from the next frame onward. Consequences: Read-your-writes: after writing an entity's transform, reads of that entity return your pending value; every other entity still reads start-of-frame state. Safe despawns: despawning an entity that later lines in the same callback still reference is harmless — the despawn lands after. Events are next-frame mail: a sent event is never visible to the sending frame — readers and handlers see it on the following frame, then it's gone. Ending the loop # How When Window closed windowed mode, automatic ctx.quit() anywhere in your callback game.quit() from outside the callback max_frames=N reached tests, CI, fixed-length demos run returns the total frame count. A Game instance runs once ; create a new one to run again. Frame pacing # target_fps=60 (default) sleeps between frames to hold 60 fps. target_fps=None runs flat out — for headless tools and benchmarks. vsync=True (default) additionally syncs presentation to the display. Windowed games still render every frame even when idle; there is no event-driven redraw mode yet. Headless mode # Copy The identical loop without window, GPU, or OS input events. Physics, events, resources, and your logic all run; MeshRenderer components are inert (no GPU exists to materialize them). See Testing . Errors # Engine failures (lost GPU device, window errors) raise automata.Error . Exceptions raised inside your callback stop the loop and propagate unchanged out of run — wrap in try/except if you want cleanup. See also # Scenes & Entities — what the loop updates Rendering & Lights — what the loop draws Testing — headless patterns"},{"t":"Guide: Scenes & Entities","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/scenes-and-entities.html","h":[["staging-a-scene","Staging a scene"],["the-components","The components"],["entity-classes-reusable-archetypes","Entity classes: reusable archetypes"],["at-runtime-sceneview-and-entityview","At runtime: SceneView and EntityView"],["patterns","Patterns"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: Scenes & Entities AUTOMATA is an ECS engine with a Pythonic face: entities are bags of components , and a scene is the set of entities alive in your world. You never touch the ECS directly — you stage plain Python builders and the engine materializes them. Staging a scene # Copy Entity(\"name\") — the name is optional but is how you look the entity up at runtime. Methods chain: .add(component) returns the entity. Scene().add(entity) returns the scene — build the whole level in one expression, or subclass (below). load_scene clears the old world and spawns the new one. Call it before run , or inside on_update to switch levels. The components # Component Purpose Transform(position, rotation_euler, scale) Where it is, how it's turned, how big MeshRenderer.cube(size, color) / .sphere(...) / .quad(...) A visible 3D primitive (plus optional roughness , metallic , emissive ) Camera3D(position, target, fov) / Camera2D(center, half_height) The frame's eye (first entity wins) DirectionalLight(direction, color, ambient, shadows) The frame's sun (first entity wins) RigidBody.dynamic()/.fixed()/.kinematic_velocity()/.kinematic_position() How physics moves it Collider3D.cuboid(x,y,z) / .ball(r) / .capsule(h,r) / .mesh(model) / .convex_hull(model) Its 3D collision shape Collider2D.cuboid(x,y) / .ball(r) / .capsule(h,r) Its 2D collision shape CharacterController(...) Kinematic character solver (slopes, steps, snap) CharacterVelocity(velocity) The per-frame drive input for that solver Unknown component types are rejected with a TypeError listing the supported set. Entity classes: reusable archetypes # Subclass Entity to bundle a reusable thing. This is the Django-models habit: define once, place everywhere. Copy Constructor rule: the first positional argument of an Entity constructor is always the entity name . Give subclass constructors keyword parameters ( Coin(f\"coin_{i}\", x=x, y=y) ), or a positional like 2.0 is interpreted as a name and rejected. At runtime: SceneView and EntityView # Inside update , ctx.scene is a snapshot with a command buffer: Copy EntityView is the per-entity handle: Member Read Write .id / .name yes — .transform TransformRef (live) assign a Transform , or set .position / .rotation_euler / .scale on the ref .character_velocity (vx, vy, vz) assign a tuple — the character drive .camera_3d Camera3D copy assign a Camera3D — follow cameras .despawn() — queues removal All writes buffer and apply after your callback (see The Game Loop ). Patterns # Find once, cache the id. scene[\"player\"] does a name scan; fine per-frame for a handful of lookups. For hot loops over many entities, collect views once per frame with for view in scene . Spawn during play. ctx.scene.spawn(entity) is how projectiles, drops, and waves appear. The entity exists from the next frame. Level switching. self.load_scene(Level2()) inside on_update or an on_<event> handler swaps the world when the frame's callback returns: the next frame sees only the new level. Other writes to the old level's entities that frame are dropped, and anything you spawn that frame lands in the new level. (In plain-function style, ctx.scene.load(Level2()) does the same.) See also # Rendering & Lights — cameras, meshes, suns Physics — bodies and colliders in depth OOP Style — Game / Scene / Entity subclassing rules API Reference"},{"t":"Guide: Rendering & Lights","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/rendering-and-lights.html","h":[["drawing-primitives","Drawing primitives"],["surfaces","Surfaces"],["glowing-objects-light-their-surroundings","Glowing objects light their surroundings"],["cameras","Cameras"],["the-sun","The sun"],["point-lights-and-spotlights","Point lights and spotlights"],["environment","Environment"],["sky-and-quality","Sky and quality"],["beautiful","Beautiful"],["water","Water"],["particles","Particles"],["mood-recipes","Mood recipes"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: Rendering & Lights How things get on screen: primitives, cameras, the sun, the environment (ambient, fog, tone mapping), and quality presets. Drawing primitives # Copy size sets the primitive's base size (a sphere's diameter); the entity's Transform.scale multiplies it. A floor is a unit cube with scale=(40, 1, 40) . quad lies in the XY plane facing +Z — billboards, cards, flat details. Colors are linear-space floats in 0.0..=1.0 . Primitives are plain data : safe to stage before a window exists and inert in headless mode. The windowed driver uploads them to the GPU on the first frame automatically. Python has no texture or custom-mesh API yet. For textured, detailed objects, load a model ( MeshRenderer.model ), see Models . Surfaces # Colour says what a thing is; the surface says what it's made of . Every MeshRenderer constructor takes four keyword-only surface arguments: Copy Argument Range Effect roughness 0..1 0 is a mirror-like gloss with a tight, bright highlight; 1 is chalky with no visible highlight. Default 0.6 metallic 0 or 1 (in between for dusty / painted metal) Metals have no diffuse colour: they reflect the sky and sun tinted by color . Default 0 emissive (r, g, b), ≥ 0 Light the surface gives off itself, unaffected by sun, shadow or fog darkness emissive_strength ≥ 0 Multiplies emissive . Above about 1 it blooms on Ultra and Beautiful glow_light ≥ 0 How much the glow lights nearby surfaces (see below). 1 by default, 0 turns it off glow_shadows True / False Whether objects block that light and cast shadows from it (High and up). True by default Tips: Metals look best with a bright, saturated base colour: gold (1.0, 0.77, 0.34) , copper (0.95, 0.64, 0.54) , silver (0.95, 0.95, 0.95) . They need a sky to reflect, so pair them with an Environment. Shiny surfaces show their shape best on round things. A glossy cube only lights up when a face catches the sun. Models keep their own materials: glTF base colour, roughness, metallic, normal maps, emission ( KHR_materials_emissive_strength too) and texture maps are all imported. Passing roughness / metallic / emissive overrides every part of the model. Every quality level shades surfaces; Beautiful uses the full physically based model, Low to Ultra a cheaper approximation that looks close. Glowing objects light their surroundings # Anything that glows also lights the things around it, in its own colour: a lamp sphere throws a warm pool on the floor and the wall behind it, glowing coins light the ground under them, a neon sign tints the street. Nothing to set up: the engine turns every glowing mesh into a light at its center. Copy How bright: as bright as a real glowing object of that size and brightness. A bigger or brighter glow lights more, and farther. The light fades with the square of the distance and stops where it's too dim to see. glow_light scales it: 0 glows without lighting anything (screens, UI-like signs, distant windows), 2 – 5 for small things that should still throw a visible pool, like the coins in Coin Run. Night and dusk show it best. In daylight the sun outshines all but the brightest glows. Models: glowing parts of a model light too. An emissive texture lights in proportion to how much of it is lit (a few windows light less than a whole glowing wall). Shadows: on High and up, objects block glow light: crates around a lantern throw long shadows outward across the floor, and a pillar between a lamp and a wall leaves its outline on the wall. The glowing mesh itself never blocks its own light. glow_shadows=False (Oberon: untick Glow Shadows ) lets a glow's light pass through everything, which is cheaper; use it for small or unimportant glows. Limits: a frame shades at most 4 (Low), 8 (Medium), 16 (High) or 32 (Ultra, Beautiful) glow lights (placed lights, below, count too); with more, the ones nearest the camera win. Of those, the nearest 2 (High) or 4 (Ultra, Beautiful) that have glow_shadows on cast shadows. The rest light through walls, and so does every glow on Low and Medium. Cameras # Copy The first entity with a camera component is the frame's camera (3D for meshes, 2D for sprites). No camera → meshes fall back to (0, 0, 5) looking at the origin; sprites fall back to screen-space pixels, origin top-left, +Y down. Follow camera — cameras are writable per frame, so tracking a player is three lines (smooth exponential follow): Copy The sun # Copy The first DirectionalLight entity lights the frame. No light entity → a neutral shadowless default (the scene is still visible). Meshes are Blinn-Phong shaded: ambient * color everywhere, plus sun-colored diffuse and a subtle specular highlight where lit. Shadows are shadow-mapped automatically, so any level size works. On High and up the view is split into cascades (2 on High, 3 on Ultra, 4 on Beautiful): the first covers the few metres around the camera at full resolution, so shadows under the player are sharp, and each next one covers farther, coarser. Cascade edges blend, and shadows fade out at shadow_distance . A small level is covered only as far as it reaches, so it gets even sharper shadows. Medium uses one map around the whole scene (or the view, if that's smaller). Shadowed regions keep the ambient term. shadow_distance (metres, default 120): how far shadows reach. Shorter is sharper: a tight indoor or top-down game can use 30–50. shadow_softness (default 1): how soft shadow edges are. 0 is as sharp as the shadow map allows, higher values blur the edge (Beautiful shows the most difference; High and Ultra stay crisp). On Beautiful, shadows are sharp where an object touches the ground and soften with the distance to it, as much as the sun's size ( sun_size ) times the softness says: a pole's shadow starts crisp at its foot and blurs toward its tip. Point lights and spotlights # Place lights anywhere: a torch on a wall, a lamp post, a car's headlights, a flashlight. A PointLight shines every way from its entity's position. A SpotLight shines in a cone alon"},{"t":"Guide: Cameras","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/cameras.html","h":[["the-rules","The rules"],["camera3d","Camera3D"],["camera2d","Camera2D"],["follow-camera","Follow camera"],["orbit-camera","Orbit camera"],["camera-rail-cutscenes","Camera rail (cutscenes)"],["screen-shake","Screen shake"],["zoom","Zoom"],["several-cameras","Several cameras"],["behaviour-scripts","Behaviour scripts"]],"x":"Guide: Cameras How the engine picks a view, the two camera types, and the patterns almost every game needs: follow, orbit, rails, shake, and zoom. If you build scenes in Oberon, read Oberon: Cameras as well. It covers the editor tools (frustums, preview, Look Through) and the entity.camera scripting helper. The rules # A camera is a component . Put at.Camera3D or at.Camera2D on any entity. The first entity with a Camera3D is the view for meshes. The first with a Camera2D is the view for sprites. No Camera3D means meshes are seen from (0, 0, 5) looking at the origin. No Camera2D means sprites use screen pixels. Cameras are plain values. To move one, assign a new camera every frame. It applies after your update, like every other write. Camera3D # Copy Argument Meaning position Where the eye is target The point it looks at. \"Up\" is always world +Y fov Vertical field of view in degrees The aspect ratio follows the window automatically. Near and far clip are 0.1 and 1000 when built from Python; Oberon lets you edit them. Camera2D # Copy half_height is how many world units fit between the screen centre and its top edge. Halve it to zoom in 2×. Follow camera # The basic pattern stores the camera state on your game object, eases it toward a goal every frame, and assigns a new camera. Copy Why 1 - exp(-rate * dt) and not rate * dt ? It gives the same feel at 30 fps and at 240 fps, and it can never overshoot. Orbit camera # Copy To make movement camera-relative, rotate the input by the yaw: Copy Camera rail (cutscenes) # Move along a list of keyframes with smooth easing: Copy Screen shake # Shake the target a little, not the position. It reads as impact without making the player feel ill. Copy Zoom # Animate fov : about 60 normally, 30–40 when aiming, 75+ while sprinting for a sense of speed. Copy Several cameras # Only the first camera renders. To cut between views, mark each shot with an empty entity: its position is the eye, and a Vec3 attribute look holds the target. Then copy the shot you want into the main camera: Copy Behaviour scripts # Scripts attached in Oberon (and run with at.run_scene ) get a helper that does the bookkeeping for you: Copy See Oberon: Cameras ."},{"t":"Guide: Input","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/input.html","h":[["the-snapshot","The snapshot"],["action-maps-the-layer-youll-actually-use","Action maps: the layer you'll actually use"],["binding-kinds","Binding kinds"],["gamepads","Gamepads"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: Input Keyboard, mouse, and gamepads — polled raw for toys, bound to named actions for games. The snapshot # ctx.input is the device state at the start of the frame: Copy Key names are typed enums — at.Key.SPACE , at.Key.ARROW_LEFT , at.Key.F1 — that your IDE autocompletes. Every enum member is a plain str , so \"space\" works identically; prefer enums for typo safety. Headless mode receives no OS input events — everything reads as unpressed. Drive headless tests through physics and logic instead (see Testing ). Action maps: the layer you'll actually use # Copy Rules that matter: Bindings stack. Three bind_axis(\"move_x\", ...) calls mean A/D, arrows, and the stick all work at once; values sum, clamped to −1..1. (This was once a replace-not-stack bug — if your game only jumps but never walks, update your checkout.) Query against ctx.input — the map itself is stateless, so it works with the frame snapshot. Remapping is game code — rebind(action, [bindings]) , unbind(action) , unbind_axis(name) work at runtime; a settings screen is ordinary game code. Binding kinds # Constructor Triggers on Binding.key(key) keyboard key Binding.mouse(button) mouse button Binding.gamepad_button(button) any connected pad's button Binding.gamepad_axis_positive(axis) / ..._negative(axis) analog axis past ±0.5 (triggers-as-buttons) AxisBinding.key_pair(neg, pos) two keys → −1/0/+1 AxisBinding.button_pair(neg, pos) two bindings → −1/0/+1 AxisBinding.gamepad_axis(axis, scale=1.0) analog value × scale ( -1.0 inverts) Gamepads # Copy Buttons: SOUTH/EAST/NORTH/WEST (A/B/Y/X or Cross/Circle/Triangle/Square), triggers, LEFT_THUMB / RIGHT_THUMB (stick clicks), d-pad. Axes: LEFT_STICK_X/Y , RIGHT_STICK_X/Y , and more — all in GamepadButton / GamepadAxis . See also # The Game Loop — where the snapshot comes from Cookbook — double-tap dash, input buffering API Reference"},{"t":"Guide: Physics","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/physics.html","h":[["bodies","Bodies"],["colliders","Colliders"],["colliders-from-models","Colliders from models"],["the-per-frame-toolbox","The per-frame toolbox"],["water","Water"],["ripples-and-wakes","Ripples and wakes"],["the-character-controller","The character controller"],["common-pitfalls","Common pitfalls"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: Physics Rapier-powered rigid bodies in 3D and 2D, a character controller, and raycasts — stepped at a fixed 60 Hz so gameplay is identical at any frame rate. Bodies # Copy Fine-tuning: `at.RigidBody(body_type=\"dynamic\", gravity_scale=1.0, linear_damping=0.0, angular_damping=0.0, enabled=True)`. Colliders # Copy Chainable material modifiers: .friction(0.8) , .restitution(0.3) (bounciness), .density(2.0) , .sensor(True) (detect overlap without blocking movement — pickups, triggers). Collider sizes are full sizes. Transform(scale=(40, 1, 40)) pairs with Collider3D.cuboid(40, 1, 40) . Get this wrong and things float or sink by exactly half. Colliders from models # mesh and convex_hull take a model file (relative to the scene, like MeshRenderer.model ) and build the shape from it when the scene starts, after the model's import settings and at the entity's scale. The scene stores only the path. Mesh is exact: rays and characters hit the real triangles, so ramps, arches and bevelled platforms behave as they look. Use it for static scenery (no rigid body, or a static or kinematic one). A triangle mesh has no inside, so it can't give a dynamic body mass: on a dynamic body it plays as its convex hull, and Oberon and Build Game flag it. Convex hull wraps the model like shrink-film: no dents or holes. Use it for dynamic bodies a box or capsule fits badly. Boxes, balls and capsules are still the cheapest. Prefer them when they fit. 2D and 3D are separate worlds. A Collider2D never touches a Collider3D . Pick one per game; per entity, use the matching family. The per-frame toolbox # Copy ctx.physics2d mirrors the essentials for the 2D world ( apply_impulse , grounded , body_count ). Water # Every entity with a Water component is also a volume of water. Dynamic bodies in it get two things each step: Buoyancy : an upward push of `water density × gravity × submerged volume`, applied where the body is under, so a body settles at the depth its density gives and tilts with the waves. A body floats when its collider density is below the water's, with that fraction under. Drag : it loses speed and spin in proportion to how much of it is under ( drag , default 1.0; 0 turns it off). Copy Bodies ride exactly the waves that are drawn: physics samples the same Gerstner waves on the CPU, on the same clock. A long wave over a large surface moves the geometry. Waves shorter than about four of the surface's 128 grid cells only ripple the shading, so they don't move bodies either. In Oberon, set Density and Drag in the Water section of the Inspector. It's built to be cheap: Without water it costs nothing. Sleeping bodies are skipped, and bodies that settle on calm water fall asleep. Awake bodies away from the water cost a couple of comparisons. A body in the water is sampled at 1 point if small, or 8 if not (so long hulls pitch and roll). About 0.25 µs per floating body per step on waves, measured on one core. The surface isn't solid: give the water a floor if sinking things should stop. The Lagoon example floats crates and buoys, and runs a boat round the island. Ripples and wakes # On top of the waves, a small wave simulation runs around the camera: a 256 × 256 grid, a quarter metre per cell (64 m across), that follows the view. Anything crossing the surface pushes it about, dynamic and kinematic bodies alike: A body that drops in splashes: rings spread out from it, and foam. The harder and bigger, the bigger the splash. A body that bobs sends out small rings as it bobs. A body that moves pushes ripples ahead and leaves a V-shaped wake behind it, with a trail of foam. Ripples spread at 1.6 m/s, so anything faster than that (a boat) gets a proper V. Scripts make their own: ctx.water.splash(position, strength) (scene scripts, Oberon included) or ctx.physics.splash((x, y, z), strength) (engine scripts). position can be an entity; strength runs from 0 to 10, and 1 is about a crate dropped from a couple of metres. Copy Ripples are only drawn; they don't move bodies (those ride the waves). They fade over about ten seconds. Beyond the 64 m around the view there are none, and the field fades out toward its edges so it never ends in a seam. It costs nothing until something disturbs the water and nothing again once it calms down. While it runs, about 0.25 ms per physics step in a release build. The engine and Oberon point it at the camera that draws the frame; with your own PhysicsWorld3D in Rust, call `set_ripple_view(eye, target) each frame and hand ripples() to DrawList::set_ripples`. The character controller # The platformer recipe — tight movement that still collides and climbs slopes: Copy Copy CharacterController options (keyword-only): snap_to_ground , autostep , max_slope_climb_angle , min_slope_slide_angle , offset , slide , and mode=\"normal\" (or \"autostep\" -friendly setups). Common pitfalls # Player falls through the floor: collider sizes don't match the visual scale (use full sizes), or the floor lacks RigidBody.fixed() . Kinematic character never moves: you set Transform.position instead of character_velocity — the solver only sees the velocity component. Dynamic body jitters against a wall: that's restitution/friction; try friction(0.2) on the wall or clamp velocity yourself. See also # Scenes & Entities — component basics Cookbook — coyote time, jump buffering API Reference"},{"t":"Guide: Events & Resources","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/events-and-resources.html","h":[["events","Events"],["handlers","Handlers"],["resources","Resources"],["events-vs-resources","Events vs resources"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: Events & Resources Two small systems that replace a lot of architecture: events for things that happen , resources for things that are . Events # Copy Payloads are arbitrary Python objects. Events live for one frame : the one after they're sent. Send in frame N, read in frame N+1 — then they're gone. Nothing queues longer than that — events are notifications, not a log. Any part of the game can send; any part can read. Physics collisions, pickups, \"level complete\", UI clicks — all just names and payloads. Handlers # OOP style registers methods by name automatically: Copy Decorator style: Copy Handlers run right after the update callback each frame, once per matching event readable that frame — i.e. the frame after it was sent. Resources # Copy Resources are the game's shared whiteboard: score, current level number, a seeded RNG, a reference to your save-file object. If you'd otherwise make it a module-level global, put it in resources instead — tests can then run many games without cross-contamination. Events vs resources # Events Resources Lifetime next frame only until removed Question it answers \"what just happened?\" \"what is true right now?\" Examples hit, pickup, death, level_complete score, lives, inventory, settings See also # The Game Loop — the two-frame rule in context OOP Style — handler auto-registration rules API Reference"},{"t":"Guide: OOP Style","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/oop-style.html","h":[["the-game-subclass","The Game subclass"],["entity-and-scene-subclasses","Entity and Scene subclasses"],["mixing-styles","Mixing styles"],["ide-support","IDE support"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: OOP Style Three coding styles, one engine. Pick per project — they mix freely. Style Looks like Best for OOP class Platformer(at.Game) Real games, anything you'll maintain Decorator @game.update on an instance Small toys, sketches Procedural game.run(tick) Throwaway experiments, tests This guide is the OOP style in full. The Game subclass # Copy Every option defaults to None = \"engine default\"; keyword arguments to the constructor win over class attributes: Platformer(headless=True) . Direct construction with an unknown option raises TypeError ; subclass constructors may take their own parameters freely. Hooks: on_start runs once; on_update(ctx) runs every frame; any method named on_<event> auto-registers as a handler for <event> . Entity and Scene subclasses # Copy This is the Django habit applied to games: entity classes are your models (reusable, importable archetypes), scene classes compose them into levels, and the game class is the view wiring input to behavior. The constructor rule: an Entity 's first positional argument is its name. Subclass constructors take keyword parameters ( Coin(f\"coin_{i}\", x=x, y=y) — never Coin(f\"coin_{i}\", x, y) ). Mixing styles # Everything is the same object underneath: Copy Callback resolution order: run(update=...) → @game.update → on_update override. If none exists, run raises TypeError . IDE support # The wrapper layer is fully typed — annotate your hooks and your editor autocompletes the whole frame context: Copy Components accepted by add() are the at.Component union — type checkers flag unsupported objects before you run. See also # Project Structure — one file becomes a package Events & Resources — the on_<event> contract API Reference"},{"t":"Scene Files: Saving and Loading Worlds","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/scene-files.html","h":[["what-a-scene-file-looks-like","What a scene file looks like"],["the-four-entry-points","The four entry points"],["durability-rules","Durability rules"],["current-limitations","Current limitations"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Scene Files: Saving and Loading Worlds Every scene can be written to disk as a .ron file — a plain-text, hand-editable, git-diffable snapshot of the world. This is the format the Oberon editor saves, but it works just as well for hand-authored levels, save games, and test fixtures. Copy What a scene file looks like # RON (Rust Object Notation): struct-like syntax with comments and trailing commas. One record per entity; absent components are omitted entirely. Copy Every field is exactly what the matching at.* constructor accepts — you can hand-edit values, add entities, or write whole levels by hand. The four entry points # Call Input Use it for scene.to_ron() a staged Scene in-memory snapshots (undo, play-mode restore) scene.save(path) a staged Scene writing levels from code at.Scene.from_ron(text) RON text parsing without touching the filesystem at.Scene.load(path) a file path loading levels into game.load_scene(...) And on a running game: Call Captures game.scene_to_ron() the live world — moved transforms and all game.save_scene(path) same, straight to disk The staged/lived distinction matters: scene.save() writes what you built ; game.save_scene() writes what is — after physics, scripts, and events have run. A crate staged at y=5 and saved after 240 frames is recorded at its resting position. Durability rules # version: 1 sits at the top of every file. Loading a file written by a newer engine raises ValueError instead of guessing. Unknown fields are ignored; missing fields fall back to defaults. Older and newer engines read each other's files as far as the overlapping fields allow. Malformed files raise ValueError with line/column information from the RON parser; unreadable paths raise IOError . Current limitations # Scenes are flat — entity parenting ( Parent ) is not serialized yet. MeshVisual primitives ( Cube , Quad ) serialize by tag; custom geometry works but stores raw vertices ( Custom variant). Model files serialize as Model(path: \"models/tree.glb\") , a path relative to the scene file. The model (and its textures) load from disk when the scene does. See Models . Colliders whose shape isn't centred on the entity carry an offset (omitted when zero). Sprites are not part of the format yet. Serialization captures authoring data, not runtime physics state: velocities and contacts are rebuilt on load. See also # Scenes and Entities — the staging model Project Structure — where scene files live in a growing project Oberon design — the editor that writes these files"},{"t":"Guide: Project Structure","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/project-structure.html","h":[["the-layout","The layout"],["what-goes-where","What goes where"],["the-rules-of-thumb","The rules of thumb"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: Project Structure One file is right until it isn't. When the platformer passes ~300 lines, split it the Django way: each concern in one obvious home. The layout # Copy What goes where # entities.py — archetypes, nothing else: Copy scenes.py — composition: Copy systems.py — pure functions taking (game, ctx) : Copy main.py — wiring and policy: Copy The rules of thumb # Entities are data — no update logic in entity classes; behavior lives in systems or the Game hooks. Scenes compose, never compute — level layout is a list of add calls, not procedural generation (put generators in scenes.py as separate functions if you need them). Game state on the Game or in resources — self.score for game-local state, ctx.resources for things tests and tools poke at. Constants at module top — speeds, colors, gravity. Your future self tunes games by editing exactly one block. See also # OOP Style Testing"},{"t":"Guide: Testing","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/testing.html","h":[["the-pattern","The pattern"],["probing-a-game-subclass","Probing a Game subclass"],["what-to-test","What to test"],["ci","CI"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Guide: Testing Your game is a Python program; test it like one. Headless mode runs the real engine — physics, events, resources, your logic — with no window and no GPU. The pattern # Copy Headless rules: headless=True + max_frames=N bounds every run. MeshRenderer is inert (no GPU) — stage visuals anyway; they're ignored. No OS input events arrive. Test outcomes and logic, not key plumbing: instead of \"pressing space makes the player jump,\" test \"when grounded and jump is triggered, vy becomes positive\" — or drive physics directly ( ctx.physics.apply_impulse ) and assert the landing. Probing a Game subclass # Wrap the real update hook rather than replacing it: Copy An explicit run(update=fn) also wins over on_update ; call Platformer.on_update(game, ctx) inside it to keep the real logic. What to test # Layer Example assertion Scene staging names present, entity_count right Physics ball rests at expected height after N frames Game logic collecting a coin despawns it and bumps score Events sending hit triggers your handler Action maps bindings stack; axis_names() correct API misuse bad colors raise ValueError , unknown keys raise KeyError CI # The repo's own CI runs the Python suite headless on every push — your game's tests work the same way: Copy GPU rendering is covered separately by the engine's offscreen tests (they run under lavapipe in CI); your game tests never need a GPU. See also # The Game Loop The engine's own suite: crates/automata-python/tests/python/test_automata.py"},{"t":"Guide: Performance","g":"Python guides","u":"guides/performance.html","h":[["practical-limits","Practical limits"],["the-numbers","The numbers"],["what-is-measured","What is measured"],["measure-on-your-machine","Measure on your machine"]],"x":"Guide: Performance How many scripted entities fit in a frame? This page answers that with measured numbers, explains what the numbers include, and shows how to measure on your own machine. Practical limits # At 60 FPS a frame lasts 16.7 ms, and that time is shared by your scripts, physics and drawing. On the reference machine below: Plain scripted entities cost about 15 µs each per frame in a game ( run_scene or an exported build). About 1,000 fit in a 60 FPS frame before anything is drawn. Keep to around 700 to leave room for rendering. Scripted physics characters (a character controller each) cost about 50 µs each . Keep to around 250 . Oberon Play takes 1.5 to 2 times as long per frame as the game, because every frame crosses to a separate Python process and back. A scene that runs at 30 FPS in Play can run at 60 FPS once exported. Judge final performance with run_scene or an export, not in the editor. Script time grows in proportion to the number of scripted entities, so you can scale these figures to your own scene. A faster CPU than the reference machine (a small cloud VM) will fit more. The numbers # Measured November 2026 on an Intel Xeon @ 2.60 GHz (2 threads, Linux 6.1), with Python 3.11. Each run is headless, 300 frames after 30 warm-up frames. Each frame advances exactly 1/60 s. Scene Entities Runner Avg frame p95 frame Script Engine FPS scripts 100 Oberon Play 3.16 ms 4.25 ms 1.66 ms 1.50 ms 316 scripts 100 run_scene 1.55 ms 2.26 ms 1.40 ms 0.16 ms 644 scripts 100 export 1.54 ms 2.22 ms 1.39 ms 0.15 ms 649 scripts 1,000 Oberon Play 28.67 ms 35.98 ms 15.76 ms 12.91 ms 35 scripts 1,000 run_scene 16.52 ms 20.05 ms 14.94 ms 1.58 ms 61 scripts 1,000 export 15.77 ms 18.22 ms 14.32 ms 1.45 ms 63 scripts 5,000 Oberon Play 164.65 ms 193.53 ms 86.59 ms 78.05 ms 6 scripts 5,000 run_scene 112.07 ms 132.22 ms 100.96 ms 11.11 ms 9 scripts 5,000 export 116.03 ms 137.03 ms 104.62 ms 11.41 ms 9 physics 100 Oberon Play 7.06 ms 9.27 ms 2.58 ms 4.48 ms 142 physics 100 run_scene 3.89 ms 4.87 ms 1.52 ms 2.36 ms 257 physics 100 export 4.08 ms 6.26 ms 1.62 ms 2.46 ms 245 physics 1,000 Oberon Play 78.28 ms 91.65 ms 26.79 ms 51.49 ms 13 physics 1,000 run_scene 51.43 ms 59.94 ms 18.62 ms 32.81 ms 19 physics 1,000 export 49.47 ms 56.50 ms 17.60 ms 31.87 ms 20 physics 5,000 Oberon Play 406.34 ms 443.43 ms 142.08 ms 264.26 ms 2 physics 5,000 run_scene 273.74 ms 308.76 ms 110.41 ms 163.34 ms 4 physics 5,000 export 277.75 ms 304.86 ms 112.24 ms 165.51 ms 4 The export row used a development template, which runs the game with the installed Python rather than a bundled one. That is why it matches run_scene so closely. What is measured # The scenes come from examples/bench/build_scenes.py . Every entity runs the same script, examples/bench/bench.py . Each frame it reads two attributes, moves its entity in a small circle and casts a ray down at the ground, which is what most gameplay scripts do. scripts scenes: cubes that move by writing their transform. physics scenes: capsules with a character controller, moved through entity.character_velocity , so physics work grows with the entity count too. The columns: Avg frame / p95 frame: the average frame, and the frame that 95% of frames beat. The p95 figure shows how bad the occasional slow frame gets. Script: time spent running every entity's update . Engine: everything else in the frame. That covers the physics step, character controllers, syncing the world, and in Oberon Play the trip to the script process and back. FPS: 1000 divided by the average frame time. The runs are headless , so drawing isn't included. Rendering cost depends on your GPU, resolution and graphics quality rather than on the script count. Leave part of the frame for it. Every runner advances the game by exactly 1/60 s per frame (the fixed_delta option of at.Game ). Physics therefore takes one step per frame however long the frame took, and each row shows the cost of one 60 Hz frame. A real game that falls below 60 FPS runs extra physics steps to catch up, up to four per frame, so it slows down faster than these rows suggest. Measure on your machine # Copy Run it with the Python that has automata installed. It builds the release binaries with cargo ( oberon , automata-export and automata-player ), writes the scenes to examples/bench/scenes/ , runs each one and prints the table above with your machine and the date. --out results.json also saves every frame time. A run that exceeds --seconds (default 30) of measuring stops early and reports the frames it managed. For the export runner it uses an installed export template if there is one, otherwise a development template made from the freshly built player. The runners can also be used on their own: Oberon Play: oberon --bench scene.ron --frames 300 --out stats.json runs a scene the way Play mode does, without a window. run_scene and exported games: set AUTOMATA_FRAME_STATS=stats.json and the run writes every frame's time and script time when it ends. AUTOMATA_FRAME_STATS_WARMUP sets the frames to skip (default 30). An exported game takes --headless --frames N --fixed-delta 0.0166667 . Rerun the benchmark before each release and update this page, so that a slowdown shows up as a change in the table."},{"t":"API Reference","g":"Reference","u":"api-reference.html","h":[["game","Game"],["scene--entity-staging","Scene &amp; Entity (staging)"],["transform","Transform"],["cameras","Cameras"],["visuals","Visuals"],["directionallight","DirectionalLight"],["pointlight--spotlight","PointLight / SpotLight"],["environment","Environment"],["water","Water"],["particleemitter","ParticleEmitter"],["physics-components","Physics components"],["framecontext-ctx","FrameContext (ctx)"],["sceneview--entityview","SceneView / EntityView"],["input","Input"],["physics-ctxphysics","Physics (ctx.physics)"],["audio-ctxaudio","Audio (ctx.audio)"],["input-mapping","Input mapping"],["enums","Enums"],["behaviour-scripts-automatarun_scene","Behaviour scripts (automata.run_scene)"]],"x":"API Reference Every public name in the automata package. Conventions: angles are radians ( Transform ) or degrees ( Camera3D.fov ); colors are float tuples in 0.0..=1.0 ; Vec3 = tuple[float, float, float] . Copy Game # Copy Member Type Notes run(update=None, max_frames=None) -> int method drives the loop; returns frames run; a Game runs once load_scene(scene) method replaces the world: at once before run , at the end of the frame inside update or a handler quit() method requests shutdown state property \"created\" / \"running\" / \"shutdown\" update(fn) decorator registers the per-frame callback on_event(name) decorator registers a handler for events named name OOP subclass hooks: on_start() , on_update(ctx) , on_<event>(payload) . Class attributes mirror every constructor option; kwargs win over them. Scene & Entity (staging) # Copy See Guide: Scene Files for the format and its durability rules. at.Component — the union of everything add accepts (for annotations). Transform # Copy Cameras # Copy Read-only properties: position , target , fov / center , half_height . The first camera entity in the scene wins each frame; replace per frame via EntityView.camera_3d . Visuals # Copy Plain data; materialized on the GPU by the windowed driver, inert headless. Transform.scale sizes the primitive. Surface arguments: None roughness / metallic means \"default\", which is 0.6 / 0 for primitives and the file's own material values for models. Setting them overrides every part of a model. Roughness and metallic must be in 0..=1 , and emissive values must not be negative, or ValueError is raised. A glowing mesh also lights the surfaces around it like a real glowing object of its size; glow_light scales that (0 = off). On HIGH and up, objects in the way block that light and cast shadows from it, unless glow_shadows=False . The glow added is emissive × emissive_strength ; above 1 it blooms on Ultra and Beautiful. Guide: Rendering & Lights . model loads the file right away, so a missing file raises FileNotFoundError and a broken one raises ValueError where you create the component. Relative paths resolve against the asset root. Scene.load / run_scene set the root to the scene's folder. If no root is set, the folder of the running script is used, then the working directory. The file is cached, so a thousand trees load it once. Guide: Models . DirectionalLight # Copy The scene's sun; the first entity carrying one lights the frame. Getters: direction , color , ambient , shadows , shadow_distance , shadow_softness . Without one, a neutral shadowless default is used. shadow_distance (metres, above 0) is how far shadows reach; shorter is sharper. shadow_softness (0..8, default 1) is how soft their edges are. Out-of-range values raise ValueError . Guide: Rendering & Lights . PointLight / SpotLight # Copy Placed lights. A PointLight shines every way from its entity's position. A SpotLight shines along its entity's forward axis (local −Z, turned by the entity's rotation). intensity 1 lights a surface 1 m away like a white sun overhead, falling off with the square of the distance. range (metres) ends the light; 0 works it out from the brightness. outer_angle is the cone's edge in degrees from its axis (0–80), and inner_angle is the full-strength core (default ¾ of outer_angle ). radius is the size of the glowing source. shadows lets objects block the light (High and up, nearest first). Getters have the same names. Negative values, a zero radius, or angles out of range raise ValueError . Saved as point_light / spot_light in scene files. Guide: Rendering & Lights . Environment # Copy The scene look. The first entity carrying one wins. It sets hemisphere ambient (sky above, ground below, scaled by the sun's ambient ), the background clear colour (overriding Game.clear_color ), exposure, tone mapping ( \"none\" , \"reinhard\" , \"aces\" , \"agx\" ), and exponential distance fog. sky=True draws a sky dome instead of the flat background: a gradient from background (the horizon) to zenith_color , a sun disk sun_size degrees wide where the scene's DirectionalLight shines from, a halo ( sun_glow ), clouds cover from 0 to 1, and on BEAUTIFUL a lens flare ( lens_flare strength) when the camera looks toward the sun. Also on BEAUTIFUL , ambient_occlusion (0 off, 1 default, up to 2) darkens the ambient light in creases, corners and on the ground around objects. The non-neutral presets turn the sky on. All arguments are readable as properties. Unknown tone mapping or preset names raise ValueError . It's saved in scene files. Guide: Rendering & Lights . Water # Copy A rectangle of animated water on the entity's local XZ plane, size metres wide, centred on its Transform . Waves are Gerstner waves ( wave_height crest to rest, wave_length of the main wave, travelling toward wave_direction degrees around +Y). Every quality preset draws it; BEAUTIFUL adds refraction of what's underneath, depth-based absorption ( clarity is how many metres you can see down) and shoreline foam . endless=True (on the ocean and sea presets) continues the surface, flat and calm, out to the horizon; waves, foam and physics stay within size . It's also a volume of water for physics. Dynamic bodies in it float by buoyancy ( density against their colliders' density, which defaults to 1.0, so a default box floats half under) and are slowed by drag . All arguments are readable as properties, and it's saved in scene files. Guides: Rendering & Lights , Physics . ParticleEmitter # Copy Fields (all readable as properties): emitting , rate , burst , duration , one_shot , max_particles , lifetime (min, max), shape ( \"point\" , \"sphere\" , \"box\" , \"cone\" ), shape_size , cone_angle , speed (min, max), gravity , drag , size (birth, death), size_spread , color , color_end (RGBA, over 1 glows), fade_in , fade_out , spin , stretch , blend ( \"alpha\" , \"additive\" ), lit , soft , sprite ( \"dot\" , \"spark\" , \"star\" , \"flame\" , \"puff\" ), texture , sheet (columns, rows), world_space . Pairs also take"},{"t":"Cookbook","g":"Reference","u":"cookbook.html","h":[["movement","Movement"],["double-jump","Double jump"],["coyote-time-jump-shortly-after-leaving-a-ledge","Coyote time (jump shortly after leaving a ledge)"],["jump-buffering-jump-pressed-just-before-landing-still-works","Jump buffering (jump pressed just before landing still works)"],["cameras","Cameras"],["orbit-camera","Orbit camera"],["screen-shake","Screen shake"],["spawning--timing","Spawning &amp; timing"],["spawn-waves-on-a-timer","Spawn waves on a timer"],["pickup-by-proximity-no-physics-sensors-needed","Pickup by proximity (no physics sensors needed)"],["flow-control","Flow control"],["pause","Pause"],["switching-levels","Switching levels"],["input","Input"],["rebindable-controls-screen-core-loop","Rebindable controls screen (core loop)"],["see-also","See also"]],"x":"Cookbook Small recipes for problems every game hits. Each is complete enough to paste into the capstone platformer ( examples/python/oop_platformer.py ) and adapt. The names ( WALK_SPEED , GRAVITY , JUMP_SPEED , self.controls , self.cam_x ) are the capstone's. All of them, pasted in: examples/python/cookbook_platformer.py is the capstone with every recipe on this page working together, and examples/python/tests/test_cookbook_platformer.py tests them headless: Copy When recipes combine, they share state. The finished file merges the three movement recipes into one move method, for example. Movement # Double jump # Copy self.vy <= 0.0 matters: right after a jump the player can still touch the ground for a frame, and refilling jumps_left then would allow a third jump. Coyote time (jump shortly after leaving a ledge) # Copy Jump buffering (jump pressed just before landing still works) # Copy Cameras # Orbit camera # Copy Screen shake # Copy Spawning & timing # Spawn waves on a timer # Copy Crate is the capstone's; any Entity subclass works. Pickup by proximity (no physics sensors needed) # Copy Flow control # Pause # Copy Physics keeps stepping while paused. A character's velocity stays at whatever you last wrote, so set it to zero or the player keeps sliding. Dynamic bodies (crates) keep settling. Switching levels # Copy make_level(n) is your own function returning a Scene . Send level_complete when the last coin goes, and reset per-level state (the coin table, timers) after loading. Input # Rebindable controls screen (core loop) # Copy See also # Zero to Hero — the guided path API Reference"},{"t":"Architecture","g":"Engine","u":"engine/architecture.html","h":[["layers","Layers"],["crates","Crates"],["application-lifecycle-phase-12","Application lifecycle (Phase 1–2)"],["rendering-phase-2-scope","Rendering (Phase 2 scope)"],["python-bridge-phase-5","Python bridge (Phase 5)"],["cross-cutting-conventions","Cross-cutting conventions"]],"x":"Architecture This document describes the engine's architecture as it is actually implemented. The target design is specified in build.md section 3; this file tracks what exists and why. Layers # Copy Each layer may only depend on layers below it. Python never sees Rust ownership or lifetime details; the binding layer translates between the two worlds. Crates # Crate Status Responsibility automata-core ✅ Phase 1–2 Application lifecycle, main loop, timekeeping, logging, error types, windowed/headless runtime automata-input ✅ Phase 2 Input event collection, keyboard/mouse state automata-renderer ✅ Phase 2 (context) wgpu device, surface, frame presentation; drawing in Phase 3 automata-scene ✅ Phase 4 Scenes, entities, ECS, transforms automata-physics Phase 6 Rapier 2D/3D integration automata-audio Phase 7 Kira-based audio automata-assets ✅ Models glTF 2.0 / OBJ model loading (meshes, base colour, textures), a hot-reloading cache, asset-root path resolution automata-python ✅ Phase 5 PyO3 bindings and Python API New crates are introduced only when a subsystem boundary becomes meaningful — not ahead of time. Application lifecycle (Phase 1–2) # Copy Created — Application::new(EngineConfig) initializes engine-wide services (logging) and returns. No window or GPU device exists yet. Running — Application::run(update) invokes update once per frame with an AppContext (timing, input, quit control). Two drivers implement the same contract: WindowMode::Windowed — the winit event loop drives frames ( resumed creates the window + GPU context; about_to_wait runs the frame and paces itself via ControlFlow::WaitUntil ; RedrawRequested presents). OS input events feed InputState between frames. WindowMode::Headless — a sleep-paced loop without window, GPU, or input. Used by tests, CI, and tooling. Shutdown — reached after a quit request or window close completes the current frame. An application cannot run twice; run returns Error::InvalidState . Fatal errors inside event-loop callbacks (which cannot return Result ) are captured by the driver and reported by run after the loop exits. Rendering (Phase 2 scope) # automata-renderer::RenderContext owns the wgpu surface, device, and queue. It is deliberately minimal — device/surface management, resizing, and presenting a clear color. Key wgpu 30 API notes (verified against vendored sources): Instance::new(InstanceDescriptor::new_without_display_handle()) request_adapter / request_device return Result s DeviceDescriptor requires explicit experimental_features and trace Surface::get_current_texture() returns the CurrentSurfaceTexture enum (Success/Suboptimal/Timeout/Occluded/Outdated/Lost/Validation), not a Result — transient variants are handled internally Presentation is Queue::present(surface_texture) RenderPassColorAttachment requires a depth_slice field Phase 3 adds shaders, buffers, textures, materials, and cameras here. Python bridge (Phase 5) # automata-python wraps the engine in the automata Python package (PyO3, maturin-built). The design keeps the FFI boundary free of live borrows: Game owns the Application . run(update) borrows it for the whole loop; each frame the driver builds a FrameContext from owned snapshots (time, input, scene) plus shared write buffers (scene commands, outgoing events, quit flag). The Python callback sees start-of-frame state; its writes are applied immediately after it returns and become visible the next frame. A context object that escapes its callback holds stale owned data — no dangling references are possible, and no unsafe is required. Rust errors surface as automata.Error ; exceptions raised by the Python callback stop the loop and propagate unchanged to the caller of run . The crate sets unsafe_code = \"deny\" instead of the workspace-wide forbid : PyO3 macro expansions contain unsafe impl blocks that a forbid cannot tolerate. Hand-written binding code contains no unsafe. Cross-cutting conventions # Errors — each crate defines an Error enum with thiserror and a Result<T> alias. No panics for recoverable conditions. Logging — tracing everywhere; the subscriber is installed once by Application::new , honoring RUST_LOG . Unsafe — forbidden in engine crates ( unsafe_code = \"forbid\" ). Docs — every public item carries documentation; CI enforces it."},{"t":"Development Guide","g":"Engine","u":"engine/development.html","h":[["prerequisites","Prerequisites"],["offline--sandboxed-environments","Offline / sandboxed environments"],["everyday-commands","Everyday commands"],["python-bindings","Python bindings"],["documentation-site","Documentation site"],["coding-conventions","Coding conventions"],["working-agreements-ai-assisted-development","Working agreements (AI-assisted development)"],["commits-and-branches","Commits and branches"]],"x":"Development Guide Prerequisites # Rust stable (MSRV: 1.86 ), with rustfmt and clippy Python 3 (from Phase 5 onward) Standard setup via rustup : Copy Offline / sandboxed environments # Some development sandboxes cannot reach static.rust-lang.org or crates.io . For those, the repository provides a bootstrap path that ferries a toolchain and vendored dependencies through GitHub Actions artifacts: Run the bootstrap-toolchain workflow ( .github/workflows/bootstrap-toolchain.yml ). It packages the stable toolchain (with rustfmt and clippy ) and the cargo vendor output as workflow artifacts. From the sandbox, fetch and install them: Copy The script also writes .cargo/config.toml (git-ignored) pointing Cargo at vendor/ , so builds work fully offline. Note for sandboxed sessions: only git-tracked file contents persist across sandbox sessions — the toolchain ( ~/.local/automata-rust ), vendor/ , and .cargo/config.toml are wiped between sessions. Re-run scripts/setup-offline-env.sh at the start of every session. Re-run the bootstrap workflow whenever dependencies change (it triggers automatically on changes to any Cargo.toml ). Everyday commands # Copy All of the above run in CI and must stay green. Python bindings # The automata-python crate builds the automata Python package with maturin : Copy Sandbox note: the sandbox has no libpython , so linking any target that embeds Python fails locally. Workarounds baked into the workflow: Build/clippy the bindings with PYO3_BUILD_EXTENSION_MODULE=1 (this is what maturin sets; it skips the libpython link). Run cargo test --workspace --exclude automata-python locally; the crate's Rust unit tests link libpython and therefore run on CI only. Python-side behavior is covered locally by the pytest suite against the maturin-built wheel, which links without libpython by design. Documentation site # The wiki and engine docs are also published as static HTML under docs/site/docs/ , generated from the Markdown by scripts/build_site.py (standard library only). The Markdown is the source of truth: after editing any page under docs/wiki/ or docs/*.md , rebuild and commit the output. Copy New pages must be added to NAV in the script to get a sidebar entry. The page styles and scripts live in scripts/site/ and are copied into docs/site/docs/_static/ . Landing page ( docs/site/index.html + docs/site/landing.js ). It is set in Michroma for the wordmark and headlines, Bricolage Grotesque for body text, and JetBrains Mono for code. All three are OFL and vendored in docs/site/fonts/ . landing.js is a tiny hidden-line 3D renderer on a 2D canvas, styled after Oberon's scene view. It draws the hero scene and the coin.py playground, whose yellow numbers are draggable sliders running the same logic as the tutorial script. It also drives the Oberon tour, which highlights regions of the screenshots in docs/assets/oberon/ . If you regenerate those screenshots with a different layout, update the data-rect values (in 1600×950 pixels) in index.html . Docs pages use editorial type (Geist with Instrument Serif for display) and one-bit dithered art. docs/site/dither.js provides the dither-to-colour \"develop\" effect on screenshots. The section banners in docs/site/img/ are exported from the same shader with scripts/site/export-art.html ; its header lists the parameters for each. Everything honours prefers-reduced-motion , and the pages work without JavaScript (the canvases simply stay empty). Coding conventions # Edition 2021 , MSRV 1.86 (bumped deliberately, not casually). No unsafe in engine crates — enforced workspace-wide. Document every public item. missing_docs is a workspace lint and CI denies warnings. Document why, not just what. Errors : libraries define a crate-local Error enum with thiserror plus a Result<T> alias. Recoverable conditions are Result s, never panics. Panics signal programmer error only. Logging : use tracing macros ( info! , debug! , …). Library code never prints to stdout/stderr directly. Dependencies : prefer mature, maintained crates (build.md section 2). Declare shared dependencies in the workspace root [workspace.dependencies] and inherit with dep.workspace = true . New dependencies require a stated reason in their commit message. Formatting : rustfmt.toml holds the canonical style. Lint policy lives in the workspace [workspace.lints] tables — exceptions are documented there, not scattered across the code. Tests : unit tests next to the code, integration tests in tests/ , doc tests for public API examples. New functionality ships with tests (build.md section 10). Working agreements (AI-assisted development) # Per build.md section 10: Implement one subsystem at a time; keep the build green after each step. Inspect existing code before modifying it; no large uncontrolled rewrites. Verify dependency APIs against official documentation. Every completed subsystem has a defined public interface and tests. Commits and branches # Imperative commit messages ( Add … , Fix … ), referencing the phase or subsystem where relevant ( phase 1: … ). main must always build. Feature work happens on branches and lands via pull request with green CI."},{"t":"API Reference","g":"Engine","u":"engine/api.html","h":[["python-api","Python API"],["example","Example"],["object-oriented-style","Object-oriented style"],["loading-scenes-made-in-oberon","Loading scenes made in Oberon"],["behaviour-scripts","Behaviour scripts"],["attributes-and-prefabs","Attributes and prefabs"],["reference","Reference"],["ownership-and-consistency-rules","Ownership and consistency rules"],["native-api-rust","Native API (Rust)"],["phase-12--automata-core-automata-input-automata-renderer","Phase 1–2 — automata-core, automata-input, automata-renderer"],["phase-3--rendering","Phase 3 — rendering"],["phase-4--automata-scene","Phase 4 — automata-scene"],["phase-6--physics-and-input","Phase 6 — Physics and Input"],["physics-model","Physics model"],["input-model","Input model"]],"x":"API Reference Python API # The Python API — AUTOMATA's primary user-facing interface — landed in Phase 5 (PyO3 bindings, crate automata-python ). Build and install everything with one script: Copy Example # Copy Object-oriented style # Game , Scene , and Entity are subclassable. Engine options are class attributes; lifecycle hooks are methods; methods named on_<event> are auto-registered as event handlers: Copy The decorator and procedural styles keep working unchanged; constructor keyword arguments override class attributes. See examples/python/oop_platformer.py for a complete game. The surface supports OO style (above), procedural style (plain functions passed to game.run(update) ), and functional style (`game.run(lambda ctx: ..., max_frames=10) ). Builders chain ( Scene().add(...).add(...)`), components are mutable ( t.position = (1, 2, 0) ), and scene views support scene[\"name\"] indexing and iteration ( for e in ctx.scene ). Loading scenes made in Oberon # The Oberon editor saves the engine's RON scene-file format (Ctrl+S writes scene.ron , then scene-2.ron , … in the directory Oberon was started from), so its files load directly: Copy Physics, meshes, lights and Character Controllers come across as authored; gameplay (input → movement, cameras) is your Python code. examples/python/play_oberon_scene.py scene.ron reproduces Oberon's Play mode (WASD/arrows, Space, Shift, follow camera) as a starting point. Behaviour scripts # Attach a script to an entity in Oberon (Inspector → Script → New… ). A script is a plain module with optional start(entity, ctx) , update(entity, ctx) and stop(entity, ctx) functions: Copy In Oberon's Play mode the scripts run in a separate Python process bridged to the editor (any python3 ; set python in ~/.config/oberon/prefs.ron to pick one). Saving a script while playing hot-reloads it, and errors show in the Console with a clickable File \"…\", line N . The same files run in the engine with: Copy entity has name , transform , character_velocity , attributes , grounded and a persistent state dict; ctx has input , time , physics , scene , log(...) (plus events , resources , quit() in the engine). Scripted characters skip Oberon's built-in WASD controller. Attributes and prefabs # Custom attributes added in Oberon's inspector (bool, int, float, text, vector, color) travel with the scene file and read like a dict — on staged entities and live ones alike: Copy Values map to bool , int , float , str , (x, y, z) and (r, g, b, a) . Prefabs ( .prefab files saved from Oberon's Hierarchy, or Prefab(entity).save(path) from code) are reusable entity templates: Copy Reference # Item Purpose Game(title, resolution, headless, resizable, vsync, log_filter, target_fps, clear_color, graphics) Owns the engine; run(update=None, max_frames=None) drives the loop, load_scene(scene) replaces the scene, quit() requests shutdown, state reports the lifecycle; @game.update and @game.on_event(name) register callbacks; clear_color=(r, g, b, a) sets the window background (default very dark blue); graphics=GraphicsQuality.HIGH picks a quality preset ( LOW disables shadows) Key / MouseButton / GamepadButton / GamepadAxis / GraphicsQuality Typed str enums for every bindable input and quality preset — Binding.key(Key.SPACE) , AxisBinding.key_pair(Key.A, Key.D) ; plain strings ( \"space\" ) keep working everywhere Scene / Entity(name) Scene staging: entity.add(component) , scene.add(entity_or_prefab) , scene.find(name) , scene.entities() ; entity.attributes (dict-like), entity.script run_scene(path, max_frames=None, update=None, **game_options) Runs an Oberon scene with its behaviour scripts ( start/update/stop(entity, ctx) ) Attributes Dict-like custom values: [key] , get , in , del , keys/values/items , update , clear , to_dict Prefab(entity_or_list) Entity template: Prefab.load(path) , save(path) , instantiate(position, name) , instantiate_all(position) , entities() Transform(position, rotation_euler, scale) Local transform; angles in radians (XYZ order) Camera3D(position, target, fov) / Camera2D(center, half_height) Frame cameras (first entity wins) MeshRenderer MeshRenderer.cube(size=…, color=…) / .quad(…) — plain-data visuals; the windowed loop materializes them on the GPU, headless runs ignore them DirectionalLight DirectionalLight(direction=…, color=…, ambient=…, shadows=True) — the scene's sun; the first entity carrying one lights the frame with Blinn-Phong shading and shadow-mapped shadows FrameContext ( ctx in the callback) time , input , scene , events , resources , quit() ctx.time delta / elapsed (seconds), frame (1-based frame number) ctx.input pressed / just_pressed / just_released(key) , mouse_* variants, cursor , scroll , modifiers , gamepads ctx.input.gamepads Connected pads: ids() , get(id) , primary() , len() , iteration; state: pressed(id, btn) / pressed_any(btn) , axis(id, ax) / axis_any(ax) ; pad.left_stick() / right_stick() ; events — this frame's gamepad events ActionMap / Binding / AxisBinding Action-based input: map.bind(\"jump\", Binding.key(\"space\")) , map.bind_axis(\"move_x\", AxisBinding.key_pair(\"a\", \"d\")) , then map.pressed(\"jump\", ctx.input) / map.axis(\"move_x\", ctx.input) ; repeated bind / bind_axis calls stack (WASD + arrows + stick can share one axis; values sum, clamped to -1..1); rebind / unbind / unbind_axis for remapping ctx.scene find(name) , [\"name\"] , spawn(entity_or_prefab, position=None) , despawn(id) , entity_count , names() , iteration; EntityView.transform get/set (attribute writes via TransformRef ), EntityView.character_velocity get/set (per-frame drive for character controllers), EntityView.camera_3d get/set (follow cameras: assign a fresh Camera3D each frame), EntityView.attributes (buffered writes), EntityView.script ctx.events send(name, payload) / read(name) — buffered: sent events are readable the next frame, then expire ctx.resources Immediate set / get / remove of arbitrary Python objects, keyed by name ctx.physics (3D) cast_ray(origin, dir, max"},{"t":"Oberon — Design Document","g":"Engine","u":"engine/oberon-design.html","h":[["1-what-oberon-is--and-is-not","1. What Oberon is — and is not"],["2-brand","2. Brand"],["3-architecture","3. Architecture"],["31-process-model-in-process-embedding","3.1 Process model: in-process embedding"],["32-why-not-an-overlay--why-not-a-code-editor","3.2 Why not an overlay / why not a code editor"],["4-engine-work-required-the-embedding-hooks","4. Engine work required (the embedding hooks)"],["41-scene-serialization--ron-decided-shipped","4.1 Scene serialization — RON (decided, shipped)"],["42-manual-stepping","4.2 Manual stepping"],["43-offscreen-frame-readback","4.3 Offscreen frame readback"],["44-full-component-read-model--writable-visuals","4.4 Full component read-model + writable visuals"],["5-panels","5. Panels"],["51-as-shipped-native-rust-ui","5.1 As shipped (native Rust UI)"],["6-play-mode-semantics-unitys-rules","6. Play mode semantics (Unity's rules)"],["7-editing-interactions","7. Editing interactions"],["8-code-changes-during-play","8. Code changes during play"],["9-project--repo-model","9. Project &amp; repo model"],["10-milestones","10. Milestones"],["11-risks--open-questions","11. Risks &amp; open questions"],["12-what-oberon-deliberately-wont-do","12. What Oberon deliberately won't do"],["13-nexa-integration-notes-verified-against-nexa-ui-140","13. nexa integration notes (verified against nexa-ui 1.4.0)"]],"x":"Oberon — Design Document Status: proposal for review · No code yet Oberon is the visual editor for AUTOMATA: a standalone desktop application in the style of Unity's editor — viewport, hierarchy, inspector, play controls — for building scenes, placing entities, and tweaking component values without touching code. It is not an in-game overlay; it is a separate app that drives the engine. Copy 1. What Oberon is — and is not # Is Is not A Python desktop app built with nexa-ui An in-game debug overlay A scene authoring + inspection tool, and a Python IDE (§3.2.1) A replacement for a general-purpose editor A client of the public engine API A fork or privileged build of the engine The place you arrange a game The place you program a game The engine stays code-first. Oberon produces and consumes scene files ; game logic remains Python in the user's editor of choice. 2. Brand # Oberon follows AUTOMATA's identity: dark white sci-fi . AUTOMATA Black ( crates/oberon/src/theme.rs ): neutral, untinted blacks — background 5, panels 12, raised controls 22, the 3D viewport pure black — with the logo's warm paper-white (242, 240, 235) as both text and accent. No blue tint anywhere; color is reserved for meaning (axis colors, warnings, errors, success, syntax). Thin 1 px borders, 5 px radii, generous spacing. Primary actions are filled paper-white with black text; everything else is outline or ghost. UX conveniences: toolbar tooltips (0.45 s), a segmented Move / Rotate / Scale control with key hints, a pill play-state badge, a command palette (Ctrl+Shift+P or the toolbar Search box — fuzzy search over every menu command, scene entities, project scripts and settings pages), Settings (Ctrl+, or the gear: Appearance / Script Editor / Python / Files; changes apply instantly and persist to prefs.ron ), and interface zoom (Ctrl+= / Ctrl+- / Ctrl+0, 60–250 %). The logo's gear-in-the-A reads as the inspector's component gear; the orbital ring is the play-mode indicator. Brand assets live in docs/assets/ . 3. Architecture # 3.1 Process model: in-process embedding # Oberon runs the engine in the same Python process as the UI: Copy One tick of the nexa loop in edit mode: game.step(dt) — physics + buffered writes apply (game logic paused). rgba = game.render_frame(viewport_size) — offscreen wgpu render, read back to CPU bytes. A nexa image widget displays rgba . Why the viewport is an image feed and not GPU interop: nexa's core is a C99 immediate-mode UI with OpenGL 3.3 + a CPU software rasterizer, while automata renders through wgpu. Sharing GPU textures between those two stacks is not realistic; the RGBA handoff through CPU memory is simple, portable, and fast enough at editor scale (§11 has the numbers). The zero-readback fallback — the engine opening its own native window beside the Oberon window — stays available if the smoke test says otherwise. Why in-process, and not an editor/game subprocess pair: Frame readback is a memcpy , not a socket stream — the viewport is just a texture handoff. The inspector reads live ECS state through the existing EntityView handles; no state-sync protocol to design or debug. Edits are ordinary engine calls through the existing buffered-write path — the editor can never desync from the engine's frame model, because it is the frame. Accepted cost: a hard crash in the engine can take the UI down. The engine already isolates Python callback exceptions (they propagate out of step() and Oberon shows them in the console panel). A subprocess mode is a possible later hardening, not MVP scope. 3.2 Why not an overlay / why not a code editor # Recorded decisions (both stand): In-game overlay editors fight the game for input, screen space, and the render pass. A separate window is the Unity model and the right model. An in-engine code editor with LSP is not worth building. Revised (2026-11): Oberon is a full IDE. It gets a native Rust code editor (tabs, Python highlighting, find/replace) with an LSP client (basedpyright / pylsp / jedi-language-server over hand-written JSON-RPC) rather than a webview or a Code-OSS fork. 3.2.1 The script editor (shipped) # Scripts open as document tabs next to the Scene tab over the center area (double-click a .py in Project, Edit Script in the Inspector, or click a File \"…\", line N traceback in the Console). While a script tab is visible the 3D view isn't rendered. Model ( crates/oberon/src/code/ ): Buffer (line vector, char columns, grouped undo/redo, Python-aware auto-indent / dedent / auto-close), an incremental line-state Python highlighter ( syntax.rs ; triple-quoted strings span lines), and the system clipboard ( clipboard.rs : X11 CLIPBOARD via x11rb, wl-copy / wl-paste on Wayland, in-process fallback). View ( ui/code_view.rs ): gutter with line numbers and diagnostic marks, current-line, selection, word-occurrence and bracket-match highlights, diagnostic squiggles with hover tooltips, find/replace and go-to-line bars, completion popup, a status line, and a Save / Don't Save / Cancel bar when closing a modified tab. Mouse: click, drag, Shift-click, double-click word, triple-click / gutter line, Ctrl-click definition. Keys: Ctrl+S / Ctrl+Shift+S (save / save all), Ctrl+W, Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Z/Y, Ctrl+X/C/V (whole line with no selection), Ctrl+D, Ctrl+/, Ctrl+Shift+K, Ctrl+L, Ctrl+] / Ctrl+[, Tab / Shift+Tab, Alt+Up/Down, Ctrl+Enter, Ctrl+Backspace/Delete, Ctrl+F / Ctrl+H / Ctrl+G, F3 / Shift+F3, Ctrl+Space (complete), F12 (definition), Ctrl+Shift+I (hover), Esc (back to the scene shortcuts). Keys the editor doesn't use (Ctrl+P, Ctrl+Q, F1) stay global. Saving is all the running game needs: the bridge's mtime hot reload (§8) picks the file up. Files changed on disk reload automatically when the tab is clean. Quitting with modified scripts asks first. Language features ( lsp.rs ): the first opened script starts a language server — language_server in ~/.config/oberon/prefs.ron (a command line, \"auto\" or \"off\" ), else the first installed of basedpyright-langserv"}];
